{ id: "2024-1", year: 2024, no: 1, subject: "geography", topic: "Climatology: heating of atmosphere",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> The atmosphere is heated more by incoming solar radiation than by terrestrial radiation.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> Carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases in the atmosphere are good absorbers of long wave radiation.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct</h3>
<p>The atmosphere is largely <b>transparent to incoming shortwave</b> solar radiation, which mostly passes through and heats the Earth's surface. The surface then emits <b>longwave (infrared) terrestrial radiation</b>, which greenhouse gases absorb. So the atmosphere is heated <b>more from below</b>, by terrestrial radiation, than directly by the Sun. NCERT: "the atmosphere is indirectly heated by the earth's radiation". Statement II is true and is in fact the reason Statement I is false.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (wrong):</b> The atmosphere is heated mainly from below by terrestrial (longwave) radiation, not directly by incoming solar radiation.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> CO2, water vapour and other greenhouse gases absorb longwave radiation well.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: how the atmosphere is heated</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Insolation</b>: incoming solar radiation, shortwave (mostly visible). Only about 14 of 100 units are absorbed directly by the atmosphere (by ozone, water vapour, dust).</li>
<li><b>Terrestrial radiation</b>: the Earth, much cooler, radiates in longwave (infrared). Absorbed by <b>CO₂, water vapour, methane, N₂O, ozone</b> (greenhouse gases).</li>
<li><b>Processes</b> by which the atmosphere is heated: <b>conduction</b> (lower layers in contact with the warm ground), <b>convection</b> (vertical transfer, restricted to the troposphere), <b>advection</b> (horizontal transfer by winds: e.g. the "loo" in north India), <b>terrestrial radiation</b>, <b>latent heat of condensation</b>.</li>
<li>That's why temperature falls with height in the troposphere (normal lapse rate 6.5°C/km): the heat source is the ground.</li>
<li>Factors controlling insolation: angle of the sun's rays (latitude), length of day, transparency of the atmosphere, configuration of land. The Earth receives most insolation in the <b>subtropical deserts</b> (cloudless skies), not at the equator.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-2", year: 2024, no: 2, subject: "geography", topic: "Climatology: troposphere",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> Thickness of the troposphere at the equator is much greater as compared to poles.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> At the equator, heat is transported to great heights by strong convectional currents.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both correct and II explains I</h3>
<p>The troposphere is about <b>18 km thick at the equator</b> and about <b>8 km at the poles</b> (NCERT). Intense surface heating at the equator creates <b>strong convection currents</b> that carry heat to great heights, lifting the tropopause. The cold poles have weak convection, so the layer is shallow.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (correct):</b> The troposphere is about 18 km thick at the equator and about 8 km at the poles.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> Strong equatorial convection carries heat high and lifts the tropopause, which explains I.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: layers of the atmosphere</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Layer</th><th>Height</th><th>Features</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Troposphere</b></td><td>8 km (poles) to 18 km (equator)</td><td>All weather, clouds, 75% of the atmosphere's mass, water vapour and dust. Temperature falls 6.5°C/km. Top = tropopause (about −45°C at poles, −80°C at the equator)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Stratosphere</b></td><td>To about 50 km</td><td><b>Ozone layer</b> (15–35 km) absorbs UV, so temperature <b>rises</b> with height. Stable, so jet aircraft fly in the lower stratosphere. Nacreous clouds</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mesosphere</b></td><td>50–80 km</td><td>Coldest (about −100°C at the mesopause). Meteors burn up. Noctilucent clouds</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Thermosphere / Ionosphere</b></td><td>80–400 km</td><td>Electrically charged layers reflect radio waves back to Earth. Auroras. Temperature rises sharply. The ISS orbits here (about 400 km)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Exosphere</b></td><td>Above about 400 km</td><td>Very thin, merges into space</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>The troposphere's thickness also varies by season (thicker in summer).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-3", year: 2024, no: 3, subject: "geography", topic: "Volcanism",
  q: `Consider the following:
<ol><li>Pyroclastic debris</li><li>Ash and dust</li><li>Nitrogen compounds</li><li>Sulphur compounds</li></ol>
How many of the above are products of volcanic eruptions?`,
  opts: ["Only one", "Only two", "Only three", "All four"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) All four</h3>
<p>Volcanic eruptions release: <b>lava</b>; <b>pyroclastic material</b> (tephra: volcanic bombs, lapilli, ash, dust); and <b>gases</b>. The gases are mostly water vapour, then carbon dioxide and <b>sulphur compounds</b> (SO₂, H₂S), plus <b>nitrogen compounds</b> (nitrogen, ammonia, NOx), argon, hydrogen, chlorine and fluorine. NCERT lists "lava flows, pyroclastic debris, volcanic bombs, ash and dust and gases such as nitrogen compounds, sulphur compounds and minor amounts of chlorine, hydrogen and argon".</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: volcanoes</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Features</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Shield</td><td>Basaltic, fluid lava, gentle slopes. Largest volcanoes</td><td>Hawaiian (Mauna Loa, Kilauea)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Composite (strato)</td><td>Alternating lava and pyroclasts, explosive, viscous andesitic lava</td><td>Fuji, Vesuvius, Etna, Mt St Helens</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caldera</td><td>Most explosive. Collapse after eruption</td><td>Yellowstone, Toba, Krakatoa</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood basalt</td><td>Huge fissure eruptions</td><td><b>Deccan Traps</b>, Columbia plateau</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mid-ocean ridge</td><td>Submarine</td><td>Mid-Atlantic Ridge (Iceland)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Effects on climate:</b> sulphur aerosols (sulphate) reflect sunlight and <b>cool</b> the Earth (Pinatubo 1991 cooled the globe by about 0.5°C; Tambora 1815 led to the "year without a summer").</li>
<li>Distribution: about two-thirds on the <b>Pacific Ring of Fire</b>. Also mid-Atlantic ridge, Mediterranean, East African Rift, hotspots.</li>
<li>India: <b>Barren Island</b> (only active volcano, Andamans), Narcondam (dormant), Dhinodhar hills (Gujarat, extinct), Deccan traps.</li>
<li>Intrusive landforms: batholith, laccolith, lopolith, phacolith, sill, dyke.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-4", year: 2024, no: 4, subject: "geography", topic: "Climatology: isotherms",
  q: `Which of the following is/are correct inference/inferences from isothermal maps in the month of January?
<ol><li>The isotherms deviate to the north over the ocean and to the south over the continent.</li><li>The presence of cold ocean currents, Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift make the North Atlantic Ocean colder and the isotherms bend towards the north.</li></ol>
Select the answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<p>In January (Northern winter), oceans are warmer than land, so isotherms <b>bend north (poleward) over oceans</b> and <b>south (equatorward) over continents</b> (1 is correct). The <b>Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift are warm currents</b>. They make the North Atlantic <b>warmer</b>, pushing isotherms north. Statement 2 wrongly calls them cold currents that make the ocean colder.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>This topic was asked in both 2024 and 2025, which shows UPSC's preference for NCERT Class XI Chapter 9 ("Solar Radiation, Heat Balance and Temperature").</li>
<li><b>Warm currents</b> (Atlantic): Gulf Stream, North Atlantic Drift, Brazil, Guinea, Antilles, Florida. <b>Cold</b>: Labrador, Canaries, Benguela, Falkland.</li>
<li><b>Warm</b> (Pacific): Kuroshio, North Pacific Drift, Alaska, East Australian. <b>Cold</b>: Oyashio/Kurile, California, <b>Humboldt (Peru)</b>, West Wind Drift.</li>
<li>Warm currents flow from low to high latitudes on the <b>western</b> margins of oceans (eastern coasts of continents). Cold currents flow on the <b>eastern</b> margins of oceans (western coasts of continents, often next to deserts: Atacama, Namib, Sonoran, West Australian).</li>
<li>The North Atlantic Drift keeps Norway's ports (Hammerfest, Narvik) ice-free, and gives western Europe mild winters.</li>
<li>Meeting of warm and cold currents gives fog and rich fishing grounds: Grand Banks (Labrador + Gulf Stream), off Japan (Kuroshio + Oyashio).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-5", year: 2024, no: 5, subject: "geography", topic: "Economic geography: cocoa",
  q: `Which of the following countries are well known as the two largest cocoa producers in the world?`,
  opts: ["Algeria and Morocco", "Botswana and Namibia", "Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana", "Madagascar and Mozambique"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana</h3>
<p><b>Côte d'Ivoire</b> (about 40%) and <b>Ghana</b> (about 15–20%) together produce about <b>60% of the world's cocoa</b>, on the Gulf of Guinea coast. Indonesia, Ecuador, Cameroon, Nigeria and Brazil follow. In 2023–24, poor harvests in West Africa (El Niño, swollen-shoot virus) pushed cocoa prices to record highs, which is why it was in the news.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Algeria and Morocco are in arid North Africa; they are not cocoa producers.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Botswana and Namibia are dry southern African countries known for diamonds and minerals, not cocoa.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Madagascar is known for vanilla; Mozambique for cashew; neither is a major cocoa producer.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: cocoa</h3>
<ul>
<li><i>Theobroma cacao</i>, native to the Amazon. A tropical crop: 21–32°C, 1,500–2,000 mm rain, shade, humidity, deep well-drained soil, within about 20° of the equator.</li>
<li>Products: cocoa butter, powder, chocolate. The Ivory Coast–Ghana Cocoa Initiative (2018) sets a "Living Income Differential". <b>EU Deforestation Regulation</b> affects cocoa supply chains.</li>
<li><b>India:</b> about 30,000 tonnes a year. Kerala, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu (often intercropped with coconut and arecanut). Directorate of Cashewnut and Cocoa Development (Kochi).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Top producers of plantation and tropical crops</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Crop</th><th>Top producers</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cocoa</td><td>Côte d'Ivoire, Ghana, Indonesia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coffee</td><td>Brazil, Vietnam, Colombia, Indonesia, Ethiopia (India about 6th–7th)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tea</td><td>China, India, Kenya (Kenya is the largest exporter)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Natural rubber</td><td>Thailand, Indonesia, Vietnam, India</td></tr>
<tr><td>Palm oil</td><td>Indonesia, Malaysia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sugarcane</td><td>Brazil, India</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cashew (raw)</td><td>Côte d'Ivoire, India, Vietnam</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2024-6", year: 2024, no: 6, subject: "geography", topic: "Indian rivers: Ganga tributaries",
  q: `With reference to the Himalayan rivers joining the Ganga downstream of Prayagraj from West to East, which one of the following sequences is correct?`,
  opts: ["Ghaghara — Gomati — Gandak — Kosi", "Gomati — Ghaghara — Gandak — Kosi", "Ghaghara — Gomati — Kosi — Gandak", "Gomati — Ghaghara — Kosi — Gandak"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Gomati — Ghaghara — Gandak — Kosi</h3>
<p>Below Prayagraj the Ganga receives from the north, in order west to east: <b>Gomati</b> (joins near Ghazipur/Saidpur, UP), <b>Ghaghara</b> (near Chhapra, Bihar), <b>Gandak</b> (opposite Patna, at Hajipur/Sonpur), and <b>Kosi</b> (near Kursela, Katihar). Strictly, the Gomati is a plains-fed (groundwater) river, not Himalayan. UPSC listed it with the Himalayan rivers anyway.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Wrong order: the Gomati joins the Ganga before (west of) the Ghaghara.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Wrong: Ghaghara and Gomati are reversed, and the Kosi joins east of the Gandak.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong: the Kosi (near Kursela) joins east of the Gandak (Hajipur–Sonpur).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: the Ganga system</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Bank</th><th>Tributaries (west → east)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Left (north)</td><td>Ramganga, Gomati, Ghaghara (with Sarda/Kali, Rapti), Gandak, Burhi Gandak, Bagmati, Kosi, Mahananda</td></tr>
<tr><td>Right (south)</td><td><b>Yamuna</b> (at Prayagraj; with Chambal, Sind, Betwa, Ken), Tons, Son (largest right-bank), Punpun, Damodar (joins the Hooghly)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Source: <b>Bhagirathi</b> (Gaumukh, Gangotri glacier) + <b>Alaknanda</b> (Satopanth glacier) meet at <b>Devprayag</b>. The Panch Prayags on the Alaknanda: Vishnuprayag (Dhauliganga), Nandaprayag (Nandakini), Karnaprayag (Pindar), Rudraprayag (Mandakini), Devprayag (Bhagirathi).</li>
<li>Length about 2,525 km (the longest river in India). Splits at Farakka into the Bhagirathi-Hooghly and the Padma (Bangladesh).</li>
<li><b>Kosi</b> is the "Sorrow of Bihar" (shifting course, floods). Damodar was the "Sorrow of Bengal".</li>
<li><b>Ghaghara</b> is the largest tributary by volume. The <b>Yamuna</b> is the longest tributary.</li>
<li>National Mission for Clean Ganga / <b>Namami Gange</b> (2014). Ganga declared the National River (2008). Ganges river dolphin is the National Aquatic Animal.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-7", year: 2024, no: 7, subject: "geography", topic: "Geomorphology: weathering",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> Rainfall is one of the reasons for weathering of rocks.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> Rain water contains carbon dioxide in solution.</p>
<p><b>Statement-III:</b> Rain water contains atmospheric oxygen.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-II and Statement-III are correct and both of them explain Statement-I", "Both Statement-II and Statement-III are correct, but only one of them explains Statement-I", "Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement-I", "Neither Statement-II nor Statement-III is correct"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) II and III are correct and both explain I</h3>
<p>Rainwater dissolves atmospheric <b>CO₂</b> to form weak <b>carbonic acid</b>, which dissolves limestone and other carbonate rocks (<b>carbonation</b>). It also carries dissolved <b>oxygen</b>, which <b>oxidises</b> iron-bearing minerals (rusting, the red and yellow colours of laterite). Both are forms of <b>chemical weathering</b> driven by rainfall.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (correct):</b> Rain causes chemical weathering of rocks.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> Rainwater dissolves CO2 to form carbonic acid, which dissolves limestone (carbonation).</li>
<li><b>Statement III (correct):</b> Dissolved oxygen in rainwater oxidises iron-bearing minerals (oxidation); both II and III explain I.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: weathering</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Processes</th><th>Where dominant</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Chemical</b></td><td><b>Solution</b>, <b>carbonation</b> (CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃, dissolves CaCO₃: karst), <b>hydration</b> (water absorbed, minerals swell), <b>oxidation</b> (iron → rust) and reduction, hydrolysis</td><td>Hot, humid regions (equatorial)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Physical / mechanical</b></td><td>Unloading and expansion (exfoliation, sheeting), temperature changes (granular disintegration), <b>frost wedging</b> (freeze-thaw), salt weathering</td><td>Deserts, high mountains, cold regions</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Biological</b></td><td>Roots, burrowing animals, lichens, human activity</td><td>Everywhere</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Weathering is <b>in situ</b> breakdown (no transport). Erosion involves transport by agents (water, wind, ice, waves).</li>
<li>Weathering forms <b>soil</b> and <b>regolith</b>, and concentrates ores (bauxite, laterite: residual deposits from leaching).</li>
<li><b>Exfoliation</b> ("onion weathering") forms exfoliation domes (Half Dome, USA). Tors in granite.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-8", year: 2024, no: 8, subject: "geography", topic: "World: North Sea",
  q: `Consider the following countries:
<ol><li>Finland</li><li>Germany</li><li>Norway</li><li>Russia</li></ol>
How many of the above countries have a border with the North Sea?`,
  opts: ["Only one", "Only two", "Only three", "All four"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Only two</h3>
<p>The <b>North Sea</b> is bordered by the <b>UK, Norway, Denmark, Germany, the Netherlands, Belgium and France</b>. <b>Germany</b> (Heligoland Bight, Frisian coast) and <b>Norway</b> qualify. <b>Finland</b> lies on the <b>Baltic Sea</b> (Gulf of Bothnia, Gulf of Finland). <b>Russia</b> borders the Baltic (St Petersburg, Kaliningrad), the Barents and White Seas, the Black Sea and others, but not the North Sea.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: seas of Europe</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Sea</th><th>Bordering countries</th></tr>
<tr><td>North Sea</td><td>UK, Norway, Denmark, Germany, Netherlands, Belgium, France. North Sea oil and gas (Brent crude benchmark). Dogger Bank fishing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baltic Sea</td><td>Sweden, Finland, Russia, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland, Germany, Denmark. Almost an "inland NATO sea" after Finland and Sweden joined. Brackish</td></tr>
<tr><td>Black Sea</td><td>Ukraine, Russia, Georgia, Turkey, Bulgaria, Romania</td></tr>
<tr><td>Barents Sea</td><td>Norway, Russia (Murmansk, ice-free due to the North Atlantic Drift)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adriatic</td><td>Italy, Slovenia, Croatia, Bosnia, Montenegro, Albania</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Connections: North Sea ↔ Baltic via the <b>Skagerrak and Kattegat</b> straits (and the Kiel Canal across Germany). North Sea ↔ English Channel via the <b>Strait of Dover</b>.</li>
<li>Rivers into the North Sea: Rhine (Rotterdam: Europe's largest port), Elbe (Hamburg), Thames, Weser.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-9", year: 2024, no: 9, subject: "geography", topic: "Indian waterfalls",
  q: `Consider the following information:
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th></th><th>Waterfall</th><th>Region</th><th>River</th></tr>
<tr><td>1.</td><td>Dhuandhar</td><td>Malwa</td><td>Narmada</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.</td><td>Hundru</td><td>Chota Nagpur</td><td>Subarnarekha</td></tr>
<tr><td>3.</td><td>Gersoppa</td><td>Western Ghats</td><td>Netravati</td></tr></table></div>
In how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["Only one", "Only two", "All three", "None"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Only one</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Row 1 (wrong):</b> <b>Dhuandhar</b> falls are on the <b>Narmada</b> at Bhedaghat near <b>Jabalpur</b>, which is in the <b>Mahakoshal</b> / Narmada valley region, not Malwa (the Malwa plateau lies further west: Indore, Ujjain).</li>
<li><b>Row 2 (correct):</b> <b>Hundru</b> falls (about 98 m) are on the <b>Subarnarekha</b> near Ranchi on the <b>Chota Nagpur plateau</b>.</li>
<li><b>Row 3 (wrong):</b> <b>Gersoppa / Jog Falls</b> (about 253 m) are on the <b>Sharavathi</b> river (Shivamogga, Karnataka), in the Western Ghats, not the Netravati.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: major waterfalls of India</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Waterfall</th><th>River</th><th>State</th></tr>
<tr><td>Kunchikal</td><td>Varahi</td><td>Karnataka (among the highest)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jog (Gersoppa)</td><td>Sharavathi</td><td>Karnataka</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dudhsagar</td><td>Mandovi</td><td>Goa</td></tr>
<tr><td>Athirappilly</td><td>Chalakudy</td><td>Kerala</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shivanasamudra</td><td>Kaveri</td><td>Karnataka (first hydro plant in Asia, 1902)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hogenakkal</td><td>Kaveri</td><td>Tamil Nadu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chitrakote ("Niagara of India")</td><td>Indravati</td><td>Chhattisgarh (Bastar)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dhuandhar</td><td>Narmada</td><td>MP (Jabalpur)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kapildhara</td><td>Narmada</td><td>MP (Amarkantak)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hundru, Dassam, Jonha</td><td>Subarnarekha, Kanchi, Gunga</td><td>Jharkhand</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nohkalikai</td><td>—</td><td>Meghalaya (tallest plunge in India)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chachai</td><td>Bihad</td><td>MP</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2024-10", year: 2024, no: 10, subject: "geography", topic: "World: mountain types",
  q: `Consider the following information:
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th></th><th>Region</th><th>Name of the mountain range</th><th>Type of mountain</th></tr>
<tr><td>1.</td><td>Central Asia</td><td>Vosges</td><td>Fold mountain</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.</td><td>Europe</td><td>Alps</td><td>Block mountain</td></tr>
<tr><td>3.</td><td>North America</td><td>Appalachians</td><td>Fold mountain</td></tr>
<tr><td>4.</td><td>South America</td><td>Andes</td><td>Fold mountain</td></tr></table></div>
In how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["Only one", "Only two", "Only three", "All four"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Only two</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Row 1 (wrong):</b> the <b>Vosges</b> are in <b>Europe</b> (France, west of the Rhine rift valley) and are <b>block mountains</b>, mirrored by the Black Forest across the Rhine.</li>
<li><b>Row 2 (wrong):</b> the <b>Alps</b> are young <b>fold</b> mountains (Alpine orogeny), not block mountains.</li>
<li><b>Row 3 (correct):</b> the <b>Appalachians</b> are <b>old fold mountains</b> (Paleozoic, Caledonian/Hercynian age), now worn down.</li>
<li><b>Row 4 (correct):</b> the <b>Andes</b> are young <b>fold</b> mountains (with volcanoes) from Nazca plate subduction.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: types of mountains</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Formation</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Young fold</td><td>Compression at convergent plate boundaries (Tertiary/Alpine)</td><td>Himalaya, Alps, Andes, Rockies, Atlas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Old fold</td><td>Earlier orogenies, now eroded</td><td>Aravalli (one of the oldest), Appalachians, Urals, Caledonians (Scotland, Norway)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Block (fault)</td><td>Land between faults uplifted (<b>horst</b>) or dropped (<b>graben</b>/rift valley)</td><td>Vosges, Black Forest, Sierra Nevada (USA), Satpura and Vindhya (partly), Harz</td></tr>
<tr><td>Volcanic</td><td>Accumulation of lava/ash</td><td>Kilimanjaro, Fuji, Mauna Loa, Cotopaxi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Residual</td><td>Remnants of erosion of old mountains or plateaus</td><td>Nilgiris, Parasnath, Girnar, Mahendragiri</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p><b>Rift valleys:</b> Rhine (between Vosges and Black Forest), East African Rift, Narmada and Tapi (India), Dead Sea, Baikal.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-11", year: 2024, no: 11, subject: "ca", caYear: "2022–23", topic: "Infrastructure: greenfield airports",
  q: `Consider the following airports:
<ol><li>Donyi Polo Airport</li><li>Kushinagar International Airport</li><li>Vijayawada International Airport</li></ol>
In the recent past, which of the above have been constructed as Greenfield projects?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Donyi Polo Airport</b> (Hollongi, Itanagar, Arunachal Pradesh): built from scratch, inaugurated November 2022. The State's first greenfield airport. Name honours the sun (Donyi) and moon (Polo), revered in local tradition.</li>
<li><b>Kushinagar International Airport</b> (UP): greenfield, inaugurated October 2021, to connect the Buddhist circuit (site of the Buddha's mahaparinirvana).</li>
<li><b>Vijayawada (Gannavaram) airport</b>: an <b>existing</b> airport (since the 1950s) that was upgraded and given international status (2017). Not greenfield.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: airports policy</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Greenfield</b> = built on undeveloped land. <b>Brownfield</b> = upgrade or expansion of existing facilities.</li>
<li><b>Greenfield Airports Policy 2008</b> (Ministry of Civil Aviation): 21 greenfield airports approved, 12 operational, including Mopa (Goa, Manohar International), Donyi Polo, Kushinagar, Durgapur, Sindhudurg, Kalaburagi, Shirdi, Pakyong (Sikkim), Kannur, Orvakal, Hollongi. Navi Mumbai (opened 2025) and <b>Noida/Jewar</b> (Noida International Airport) are major new ones.</li>
<li><b>UDAN</b> (Ude Desh ka Aam Nagrik, 2016): Regional Connectivity Scheme. India has about 160 operational airports (2025).</li>
<li>India is the 3rd-largest domestic aviation market.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-12", year: 2024, no: 12, subject: "geography", topic: "Climatology: water vapour",
  q: `With reference to "water vapour", which of the following statements is/are correct?
<ol><li>It is a gas, the amount of which decreases with altitude.</li><li>Its percentage is maximum at the poles.</li></ol>
Select the answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<p>Water vapour is a variable gas that <b>decreases with altitude</b>: about 90% lies within 5 km of the surface (1 is correct). It also <b>decreases from the equator toward the poles</b>. Warm, wet tropics hold up to <b>4%</b> by volume, while dry, cold polar areas and deserts hold <b>less than 1%</b> (2 is wrong).</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Water vapour decreases with altitude; about 90% lies within 5 km of the surface.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> It is maximum in the warm, humid tropics (up to 4%) and least at the cold, dry poles.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: role of water vapour</h3>
<ul>
<li>Absorbs part of incoming insolation and much of outgoing terrestrial radiation, so it acts like a blanket. It is the <b>most abundant greenhouse gas</b> (a feedback, not a driver: warmer air holds about 7% more moisture per °C, the Clausius-Clapeyron relation).</li>
<li>Source of all clouds and precipitation. Releases <b>latent heat</b> on condensation, which powers thunderstorms and cyclones.</li>
<li>Measures: <b>absolute humidity</b> (g/m³), <b>specific humidity</b> (g/kg), <b>relative humidity</b> (% of saturation at that temperature). <b>Dew point</b>: temperature at which air becomes saturated.</li>
<li>Forms of condensation: dew, frost, fog, mist, clouds. Fog is common in industrial areas with smoke particles (smog).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-13", year: 2024, no: 13, subject: "geography", topic: "Climate types",
  q: `Consider the following description:
<ol><li>Annual and daily range of temperatures is low.</li><li>Precipitation occurs throughout the year.</li><li>Precipitation varies between 50 cm – 250 cm.</li></ol>
What is this type of climate?`,
  opts: ["Equatorial climate", "China type climate", "Humid subtropical climate", "Marine West coast climate"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Marine West coast climate</h3>
<p>The <b>Marine West Coast (Cfb, "British type")</b> climate, found on west coasts at 40°–60° latitude, has mild winters and cool summers (<b>low annual and daily range</b>) because of onshore <b>westerlies</b> and warm currents. Precipitation is <b>well distributed throughout the year</b> (cyclonic rain), mostly <b>50–250 cm</b>, more on windward mountains. Examples: UK, northwest Europe, British Columbia, southern Chile, New Zealand, Tasmania.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Equatorial climate has heavy rain (over 200 cm) and high temperatures all year, with a tiny annual range but larger daily range.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> China type climate has hot, humid summers and cold winters, so the annual range is large.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Humid subtropical is another name for China type: summer rain maximum and a larger temperature range.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Equatorial (Af):</b> low annual range but rain above 200 cm (often 250+). The daily range is higher than the annual range.</li>
<li><b>China type / humid subtropical (Cfa):</b> east coasts at 25–35° (southern China, southeast USA, southern Brazil). Hot humid summers, so a higher annual range. Rain mostly in summer (monsoonal influence). Options (b) and (c) are essentially the same type.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Köppen's scheme (simplified)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Group</th><th>Types</th></tr>
<tr><td>A: Tropical humid</td><td>Af (rainforest), Am (monsoon), Aw (savanna)</td></tr>
<tr><td>B: Dry</td><td>BWh/BWk (desert), BSh/BSk (steppe)</td></tr>
<tr><td>C: Warm temperate</td><td>Cs (Mediterranean: winter rain), Cfa (humid subtropical), Cfb (marine west coast), Cwa (monsoon with dry winter: north India plains)</td></tr>
<tr><td>D: Cold snow-forest</td><td>Df, Dw (taiga, Siberian)</td></tr>
<tr><td>E: Polar</td><td>ET (tundra), EF (ice cap)</td></tr>
<tr><td>H: Highland</td><td>Mountains</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2024-14", year: 2024, no: 14, subject: "geography", topic: "Climatology: Coriolis force",
  q: `With reference to "Coriolis force", which of the following statements is/are correct?
<ol><li>It increases with increase in wind velocity.</li><li>It is maximum at the poles and is absent at the equator.</li></ol>
Select the answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<p>The <b>Coriolis force</b> (an apparent force from the Earth's rotation) is proportional to <b>wind speed</b> and to the <b>sine of latitude</b> (F = 2mΩv sin φ). So it grows with wind velocity, is <b>maximum at the poles</b> (sin 90° = 1) and <b>zero at the equator</b> (sin 0° = 0). NCERT states both.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: forces that move air</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pressure gradient force:</b> starts the motion, from high to low pressure. Strong where isobars are close.</li>
<li><b>Coriolis force:</b> deflects winds to the <b>right in the Northern Hemisphere</b>, <b>left in the Southern</b> (Ferrel's law). Acts perpendicular to the motion, changes direction not speed.</li>
<li><b>Frictional force:</b> slows winds near the surface (strongest at the surface, negligible above about 1–3 km, less over the sea).</li>
<li><b>Geostrophic wind:</b> in the upper air, pressure gradient = Coriolis, so the wind blows <b>parallel to isobars</b>.</li>
<li><b>Cyclones</b>: anticlockwise in the Northern Hemisphere, clockwise in the Southern. <b>Tropical cyclones don't form within about 5° of the equator</b> because Coriolis is too weak to create rotation. (Typhoon Vamei, 2001, formed at 1.5°N, an exception.)</li>
<li>Also deflects ocean currents (Ekman spiral), and gives the trade winds their NE and SE directions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-15", year: 2024, no: 15, subject: "geography", topic: "Earth's motions: day length",
  q: `On June 21 every year, which of the following latitude(s) experience(s) a sunlight of more than 12 hours?
<ol><li>Equator</li><li>Tropic of Cancer</li><li>Tropic of Capricorn</li><li>Arctic Circle</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 and 4", "2 and 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 4</h3>
<p>On <b>21 June (summer solstice)</b> the Sun is overhead at the <b>Tropic of Cancer</b> (23.5°N). The whole Northern Hemisphere has days longer than 12 hours: about 13.5 hours at the Tropic of Cancer and <b>24 hours at the Arctic Circle</b> (66.5°N) and beyond. The <b>equator always has about 12 hours</b>. The Southern Hemisphere has short days: about 10.5 hours at the Tropic of Capricorn, and 24 hours of darkness at the Antarctic Circle.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: revolution and seasons</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th>Sun overhead</th></tr>
<tr><td>21 March</td><td>Vernal (spring) equinox</td><td>Equator. Equal day and night everywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>21 June</b></td><td>Summer solstice (N. Hemisphere)</td><td><b>Tropic of Cancer</b>. Longest day in the north</td></tr>
<tr><td>23 September</td><td>Autumnal equinox</td><td>Equator</td></tr>
<tr><td>22 December</td><td>Winter solstice (N. Hemisphere)</td><td>Tropic of Capricorn. Longest day in the south</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Cause: the <b>23.5° tilt</b> of the axis and its fixed orientation (parallelism) as Earth revolves.</li>
<li><b>Perihelion</b> (closest to Sun, about 147 million km): about 3 January. <b>Aphelion</b> (farthest, about 152 million km): about 4 July. Distance barely affects seasons.</li>
<li>The Tropic of Cancer passes through 8 Indian States: Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura, Mizoram.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-16", year: 2024, no: 16, subject: "environment", topic: "Wetlands: tropical peatlands",
  q: `One of the following regions has the world's largest tropical peatland, which holds about three years worth of global carbon emissions from fossil fuels; and the possible destruction of which can exert detrimental effect on the global climate. Which one of the following denotes that region?`,
  opts: ["Amazon Basin", "Congo Basin", "Kikori Basin", "Rio de la Plata Basin"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Congo Basin</h3>
<p>The <b>Cuvette Centrale</b> peatlands in the central Congo Basin (Republic of Congo and DR Congo), mapped in 2017, are the <b>world's largest tropical peatland</b> (about 167,600 km²). They store about <b>29 billion tonnes of carbon</b>, about three years of global fossil-fuel emissions. Drainage, logging, oil exploration and drought could release it, as UNEP and researchers have warned.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Amazon has peatlands too, but the largest single tropical peatland mapped is the Cuvette Centrale of the Congo Basin.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Rio de la Plata basin (Argentina, Uruguay) is temperate, with grasslands and wetlands, not the largest tropical peatland.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: peatlands</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Peat</b>: partly decomposed organic matter that builds up in waterlogged, oxygen-poor conditions (bogs, fens, peat swamp forests).</li>
<li>Cover only about <b>3% of land</b> but store about <b>a third of soil carbon</b>, twice as much as all the world's forests.</li>
<li>Major peatlands: Canada and Russia (boreal), Indonesia and Malaysia (tropical peat swamp forests: drainage for palm oil causes haze from peat fires), Congo Basin, Amazon (Pastaza-Marañón basin, Peru).</li>
<li>Drained peat emits about 5% of global anthropogenic CO₂.</li>
<li><b>Global Peatlands Initiative</b> (UNEP, 2016). The Brazzaville Declaration (2018) on protecting the Cuvette Centrale.</li>
<li>India: peatlands in the Himalayas (Ladakh, Sikkim), Kashmir valley, Northeast. Peat is used as fuel in some areas.</li>
<li>Kikori Basin: Papua New Guinea (rainforest). Rio de la Plata: Argentina–Uruguay estuary.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-17", year: 2024, no: 17, subject: "environment", topic: "Pollutants: PFAS",
  q: `With reference to perfluoroalkyl and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) that are used in making many consumer products, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>PFAS are found to be widespread in drinking water, food and food packaging materials.</li><li>PFAS are not easily degraded in the environment.</li><li>Persistent exposure to PFAS can lead to bioaccumulation in animal bodies.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<p><b>PFAS</b> are a family of over 10,000 synthetic chemicals with very strong <b>carbon–fluorine bonds</b>, used for water, grease and stain resistance. They are called "<b>forever chemicals</b>" because they barely break down (2). They are found in drinking water, food, food packaging, dust and rain worldwide (1), and they <b>bioaccumulate</b> in blood and organs of humans and animals (3).</p>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Uses:</b> non-stick cookware (PTFE/Teflon), waterproof clothing, food wrappers (fast food, microwave popcorn), firefighting foams (AFFF), cosmetics, carpets, electronics.</li>
<li><b>Health risks:</b> kidney and testicular cancer, liver damage, thyroid disease, lower vaccine response, high cholesterol, developmental effects, low birth weight. IARC classified <b>PFOA</b> as carcinogenic (Group 1) and <b>PFOS</b> as possibly carcinogenic (2023).</li>
<li><b>Regulation:</b> PFOS (2009) and PFOA (2019) are listed under the <b>Stockholm Convention</b> on Persistent Organic Pollutants (India has not ratified the PFOS/PFOA amendments). USA set the first national drinking-water limits for PFAS (2024). EU plans a broad restriction.</li>
<li>India: studies found PFAS in Chennai and other cities' water. No specific PFAS standards in BIS drinking water norms yet.</li>
<li>Other persistent chemicals in news: microplastics, DDT, dioxins, PCBs, endocrine disruptors (BPA, phthalates).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-18", year: 2024, no: 18, subject: "environment", topic: "Ecology: parasitoids",
  q: `Consider the following:
<ol><li>Carabid beetles</li><li>Centipedes</li><li>Flies</li><li>Termites</li><li>Wasps</li></ol>
Parasitoid species are found in how many of the above kind of organisms?`,
  opts: ["Only two", "Only three", "Only four", "All five"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Only three</h3>
<p>A <b>parasitoid</b> is an organism (usually an insect larva) that lives in or on a single host and eventually <b>kills</b> it, unlike a true parasite, which usually doesn't. Parasitoids are found mainly among <b>wasps</b> (Hymenoptera: ichneumonids, braconids, chalcids, Trichogramma), <b>flies</b> (Diptera: tachinid flies) and some <b>beetles</b> (Coleoptera: some <b>carabid</b> ground beetles such as <i>Lebia</i> and <i>Brachinus</i> larvae, rove beetles). <b>Centipedes</b> are predators. <b>Termites</b> are detritivores (wood feeders).</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: ecological interactions</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Interaction</th><th>Effect (A / B)</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Predation</td><td>+ / − (kills)</td><td>Tiger–deer, centipede–insects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parasitism</td><td>+ / − (usually not killed)</td><td>Tapeworm, Cuscuta, ticks</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Parasitoidism</b></td><td>+ / − (host killed)</td><td>Ichneumon wasp larvae in caterpillars</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mutualism</td><td>+ / +</td><td>Fig and fig wasp, lichens, mycorrhiza</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commensalism</td><td>+ / 0</td><td>Orchid on mango branch, barnacles on whales</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amensalism</td><td>− / 0</td><td>Penicillium inhibiting bacteria</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competition</td><td>− / −</td><td>Species sharing a resource</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Biological pest control</b> uses parasitoids: <b>Trichogramma</b> wasps (egg parasitoids) against sugarcane borers and cotton bollworms are released widely in India (NBAIR, Bengaluru). Tachinid flies, braconid wasps.</li>
<li>Integrated Pest Management (IPM) reduces chemical pesticide use.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-19", year: 2024, no: 19, subject: "science", topic: "Botany: legumes",
  q: `Consider the following plants:
<ol><li>Groundnut</li><li>Horse-gram</li><li>Soybean</li></ol>
How many of the above belong to the pea family?`,
  opts: ["Only one", "Only two", "All three", "None"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) All three</h3>
<p>The pea family is <b>Fabaceae (Leguminosae)</b>, the legumes. <b>Groundnut</b> (<i>Arachis hypogaea</i>), <b>horse-gram</b> (<i>Macrotyloma uniflorum</i>, "kulthi") and <b>soybean</b> (<i>Glycine max</i>) are all legumes, with pods and root nodules containing <b>Rhizobium</b> bacteria that fix atmospheric nitrogen.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Groundnut (Arachis hypogaea) is a legume of the pea family (Fabaceae).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Horse-gram (kulthi) is a legume.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Soybean (Glycine max) is a legume.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: legumes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Nitrogen fixation:</b> Rhizobium in root nodules converts N₂ into ammonia using the enzyme <b>nitrogenase</b> (needs anaerobic conditions; <b>leghaemoglobin</b> gives nodules a pink colour and mops up oxygen). It enriches soil, so legumes are used in crop rotation and intercropping.</li>
<li>Other legumes: pulses (chickpea, pigeon pea/arhar, lentil, moong, urad, rajma), peas, beans, clover, alfalfa (lucerne), fenugreek, tamarind, Indian laburnum, acacias, and trees like Pongamia, Prosopis.</li>
<li>Groundnut flowers above ground but the pods develop underground (<b>geocarpy</b>).</li>
<li><b>Horse-gram</b>: a drought-hardy pulse of dryland south India, called a "poor man's pulse" and a "millet-like" climate-resilient crop.</li>
<li>India is the <b>largest producer, consumer and importer of pulses</b>. Soybean: MP (Soya State), Maharashtra, Rajasthan. Groundnut: Gujarat (largest), Rajasthan, Tamil Nadu.</li>
<li>2016 was the International Year of Pulses. 10 February is World Pulses Day. Mission for Aatmanirbharta in Pulses (2025).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-20", year: 2024, no: 20, subject: "environment", topic: "Wildlife law: vermin & bats", dropped: true, ans: null,
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> The Indian Flying Fox is placed under the "vermin" category in the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> The Indian Flying Fox feeds on the blood of other animals.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],
  analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3>
<p>UPSC dropped this question. The likely reason: after the <b>Wild Life (Protection) Amendment Act, 2022</b> (in force April 2023), the separate <b>Schedule V ("vermin")</b> was <b>abolished</b>. Before that, the Indian flying fox was listed as vermin (with common crows and fruit bats). Statement I was correct under the old law but outdated under the new one, so no option was cleanly right. <b>Statement II is plainly false</b>: the Indian flying fox (<i>Pteropus medius</i>, earlier <i>P. giganteus</i>) is a <b>fruit bat</b> that eats fruit, nectar and flowers. Only the three <b>vampire bat</b> species of Central and South America feed on blood.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: WPA 2022 amendment</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reduced schedules from <b>six to four</b>: Schedule I (highest protection), Schedule II (lesser protection), Schedule III (protected plants), Schedule IV (species in the CITES appendices). Vermin schedule removed. The Centre can still declare a species "vermin" in a specific area for a period by notification (Section 62).</li>
<li>Implements <b>CITES</b> through a Management Authority and Scientific Authority. Regulates captive elephants (transfer allowed for religious purposes, controversial). Higher penalties.</li>
<li>Conservation reserves and community reserves. Invasive alien species can be regulated.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Bats and ecology</h3>
<ul>
<li>Indian flying fox: one of the largest bats, roosts in large colonies on tall trees. Important <b>pollinator and seed disperser</b> (e.g. for mahua, baobab, durian, wild banana).</li>
<li>Nipah virus reservoir: <i>Pteropus</i> bats (see Nipah notes).</li>
<li>Bats are the only true flying mammals. They use echolocation (microbats). India has about 130 bat species.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-21", year: 2024, no: 21, subject: "science", topic: "Zoology: insects",
  q: `The organisms "Cicada, Froghopper and Pond skater" are:`,
  opts: ["Birds", "Fish", "Insects", "Reptiles"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Insects</h3>
<p>All three belong to the order <b>Hemiptera</b> ("true bugs"), insects with piercing-sucking mouthparts.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Cicada:</b> famous for loud mating songs produced by tymbal organs. Some North American periodical cicadas emerge every 13 or 17 years (prime numbers). In 2024, two broods emerged together for the first time since 1803.</li>
<li><b>Froghopper (spittlebug):</b> nymphs hide in a frothy "cuckoo spit". Adults are champion jumpers relative to body size.</li>
<li><b>Pond skater (water strider):</b> walks on water using <b>surface tension</b> (hydrophobic leg hairs). A model for biomimetic robots.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> They are not birds; all three are insects of the order Hemiptera.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> They are not fish; the pond skater lives on the water surface but is an insect.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> They are not reptiles.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: insects</h3>
<ul>
<li>Class Insecta (phylum Arthropoda): 3 body parts (head, thorax, abdomen), <b>6 legs</b>, usually 2 pairs of wings, 1 pair of antennae, compound eyes, exoskeleton of chitin. The largest animal class (about 1 million described species).</li>
<li>Major orders: Coleoptera (beetles, the largest order), Lepidoptera (butterflies, moths), Hymenoptera (bees, wasps, ants), Diptera (flies, mosquitoes), Hemiptera (bugs, cicadas, aphids), Orthoptera (grasshoppers, locusts), Odonata (dragonflies), Isoptera/Blattodea (termites, cockroaches).</li>
<li>Insect decline ("insect apocalypse") from pesticides, habitat loss and light pollution threatens pollination.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-22", year: 2024, no: 22, subject: "environment", topic: "Plastics: chewing gum",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> Many chewing gums found in the market are considered a source of environmental pollution.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> Many chewing gums contain plastic as gum base.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both correct and II explains I</h3>
<p>Most modern chewing gums use a <b>synthetic gum base</b> made of polymers like <b>polyisobutylene</b> (also used in tyre inner tubes) and <b>polyvinyl acetate</b> (a plastic, also in glues). These don't biodegrade. Discarded gum sticks to pavements, is costly to remove, and breaks into microplastics, so it is a litter and pollution problem. The plastic base is <i>why</i> gum is a pollutant.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (correct):</b> Discarded gum litters streets, does not biodegrade and adds to microplastic pollution.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> Many gums use synthetic polymers (polyisobutylene, polyvinyl acetate) as gum base, which explains I.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Traditional gum was made from natural <b>chicle</b> (sapodilla tree latex) or other tree resins. Some brands are returning to biodegradable natural bases.</li>
<li>Singapore has banned chewing gum sales since 1992. UK councils spend millions cleaning gum.</li>
<li>A 2025 study found each piece of gum can release hundreds of microplastic particles into the mouth.</li>
<li>India's plastic rules: single-use plastic ban (19 items, 2022). Chewing gum isn't covered by the ban.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-23", year: 2024, no: 23, subject: "environment", topic: "Biogeography: endemic animals",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th></th><th>Country</th><th>Animal found in its natural habitat</th></tr>
<tr><td>1.</td><td>Brazil</td><td>Indri</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.</td><td>Indonesia</td><td>Elk</td></tr>
<tr><td>3.</td><td>Madagascar</td><td>Bonobo</td></tr></table></div>
How many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["Only one", "Only two", "All three", "None"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) None</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Indri</b>: the largest living <b>lemur</b>, endemic to <b>Madagascar</b>. Not Brazil.</li>
<li><b>Elk</b>: a large deer of cold temperate regions. The North American elk (wapiti) and the Eurasian elk (moose) live in North America, northern Europe and northern Asia, not tropical Indonesia.</li>
<li><b>Bonobo</b> (pygmy chimpanzee): a great ape found <b>only in DR Congo</b>, south of the Congo river. Not Madagascar.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: animals and their native ranges</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Region</th><th>Endemic / characteristic animals</th></tr>
<tr><td>Madagascar</td><td>Lemurs (indri, aye-aye, ring-tailed), fossa, tenrecs, chameleons (about half the world's species)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Australia</td><td>Kangaroo, koala, platypus and echidna (egg-laying monotremes), wombat, Tasmanian devil, emu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indonesia</td><td><b>Komodo dragon</b>, orangutan (Borneo, Sumatra), Javan rhino, Sumatran tiger, babirusa</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brazil / Amazon</td><td>Jaguar, capybara, sloths, anaconda, pink river dolphin (boto), golden lion tamarin</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Africa</td><td>Bonobo, gorilla, okapi, chimpanzee</td></tr>
<tr><td>China</td><td>Giant panda, golden snub-nosed monkey</td></tr>
<tr><td>Galápagos</td><td>Giant tortoise, marine iguana</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p><b>Wallace Line</b>: the biogeographic boundary between Asian fauna (Bali, Borneo) and Australian fauna (Lombok, Sulawesi).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-24", year: 2024, no: 24, subject: "ir", topic: "International organisations: sanitation",
  q: `Consider the following statements regarding World Toilet Organization:
<ol><li>It is one of the agencies of the United Nations.</li><li>World Toilet Summit, World Toilet Day and World Toilet College are the initiatives of this organization, to inspire action to tackle the global sanitation crisis.</li><li>The main focus of its function is to grant funds to the least developed countries and developing countries to achieve the end of open defecation.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["2 only", "3 only", "1 and 2", "2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 only</h3>
<p>The <b>World Toilet Organization (WTO)</b> is a <b>global non-profit</b> founded in <b>2001 in Singapore</b> by Jack Sim ("Mr Toilet"). It is <b>not a UN agency</b> (1 is wrong). Its initiatives are the <b>World Toilet Summit</b>, <b>World Toilet Day</b> (19 November, the founding day, adopted by the <b>UN General Assembly in 2013</b>) and the <b>World Toilet College</b> (2). It focuses on advocacy, awareness, training and market-based solutions, <b>not grant funding</b> to countries (3 is wrong).</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> It is a global non-profit founded in Singapore (2001), not a UN agency.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> World Toilet Summit, World Toilet Day (19 November) and World Toilet College are its initiatives.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> It focuses on advocacy, training and market solutions, not on granting funds to countries.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: sanitation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>SDG 6.2</b>: access to adequate and equitable sanitation and hygiene for all, and end open defecation by 2030. About 3.5 billion people lack safely managed sanitation (WHO/UNICEF JMP).</li>
<li><b>India:</b> <b>Swachh Bharat Mission</b> (2 October 2014). SBM-Gramin: India declared <b>ODF</b> on 2 October 2019 (over 10 crore toilets). <b>SBM-G Phase II</b> (2020–25): ODF Plus (solid and liquid waste management). <b>SBM-Urban 2.0</b> (2021–26): garbage-free cities. Swachh Survekshan rankings (Indore has topped repeatedly).</li>
<li>Other days: World Water Day (22 March), Global Handwashing Day (15 October).</li>
<li>Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation's "Reinvent the Toilet Challenge".</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-25", year: 2024, no: 25, subject: "environment", topic: "Big cats: behaviour",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Lions do not have a particular breeding season.</li><li>Unlike most other big cats, cheetahs do not roar.</li><li>Unlike male lions, male leopards do not proclaim their territory by scent marking.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> lions breed <b>year-round</b>, with no fixed breeding season. Females come into oestrus every few weeks if not pregnant.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>cheetahs cannot roar</b>. They purr, chirp, growl and hiss. Only the four <i>Panthera</i> species (lion, tiger, leopard, jaguar) roar, thanks to a special larynx and hyoid structure. (The snow leopard is a <i>Panthera</i> but does not roar.)</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> male leopards <b>do</b> mark territory with <b>scent</b> (urine spraying), scrapes and tree scratching, like lions and tigers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: India's big cats</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Cat</th><th>Status (IUCN)</th><th>Where in India</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tiger</td><td>Endangered</td><td>About 3,682 (2022). MP, Karnataka, Uttarakhand lead. 58 tiger reserves</td></tr>
<tr><td>Asiatic lion</td><td>Endangered (as a population, under <i>Panthera leo</i>, Vulnerable)</td><td>Only in Gir landscape, Gujarat. 891 lions (2025 census). Project Lion (2020)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leopard</td><td>Vulnerable</td><td>About 13,874 (2022). MP has the most</td></tr>
<tr><td>Snow leopard</td><td>Vulnerable</td><td>Himalaya: about 718 (SPAI 2023; Ladakh has the most)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clouded leopard</td><td>Vulnerable</td><td>Northeast (State animal of Meghalaya)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cheetah</td><td>Vulnerable</td><td>Extinct in India in 1952. <b>Project Cheetah</b> (2022): African cheetahs reintroduced to <b>Kuno NP</b>, then Gandhi Sagar WLS (MP)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p><b>International Big Cat Alliance</b> (IBCA, launched by India in April 2023 at 50 years of Project Tiger): seven big cats (tiger, lion, leopard, snow leopard, cheetah, jaguar, puma). HQ in India.</p>` }

