window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2024-76", year: 2024, no: 76, subject: "polity", topic: "Fundamental Rights: privacy",
  q: `Under which of the following Articles of the Constitution of India, has the Supreme Court of India placed the Right to Privacy?`,
  opts: ["Article 15", "Article 16", "Article 19", "Article 21"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Article 21</h3>
<p>In <b>Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India (2017)</b>, a <b>nine-judge bench</b> unanimously held that the <b>right to privacy</b> is a fundamental right, intrinsic to the <b>right to life and personal liberty under Article 21</b>, and also part of the freedoms in Part III (Art. 14, 19). It overruled <b>M.P. Sharma (1954)</b> and <b>Kharak Singh (1962)</b>, which had denied a constitutional privacy right.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Article 15 prohibits discrimination; it is not where privacy was placed.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Article 16 guarantees equality in public employment.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Article 19 freedoms support privacy, but the Court located it chiefly in Article 21.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Article 21's expanding scope</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A.K. Gopalan (1950)</b>: narrow "procedure established by law" → <b>Maneka Gandhi (1978)</b>: procedure must be "just, fair and reasonable", importing American-style due process. Since then, Art. 21 has absorbed many rights.</li>
<li>Rights read into Art. 21: livelihood (Olga Tellis, 1985), clean environment (Subhash Kumar, 1991; M.C. Mehta), health and medical care, speedy trial (Hussainara Khatoon), free legal aid, shelter, dignity, education (Mohini Jain, Unni Krishnan → Art. 21A), right against solitary confinement, handcuffing and custodial torture (D.K. Basu), privacy (Puttaswamy), <b>die with dignity / passive euthanasia</b> (Common Cause, 2018), sexual orientation (Navtej Johar, 2018), <b>right against adverse effects of climate change</b> (M.K. Ranjitsinh, 2024).</li>
<li><b>Privacy's limits</b> (Puttaswamy test): legality (a law), legitimate aim, proportionality, procedural safeguards.</li>
<li><b>Aftermath:</b> Aadhaar case (Puttaswamy II, 2018) upheld Aadhaar with limits. <b>Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023</b> (Rules notified November 2025): Data Protection Board, consent-based processing, rights of data principals.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-77", year: 2024, no: 77, subject: "science", topic: "Defence: CDS",
  q: `What are the duties of the Chief of Defence Staff (CDS) as Head of the Department of Military Affairs?
<ol><li>Permanent Chairman of Chiefs of Staff Committee</li><li>Exercise military command over the three Service Chiefs</li><li>Principal Military Advisor to Defence Minister on all tri-service matters</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<p>The <b>CDS</b> (post created on <b>1 January 2020</b>, General Bipin Rawat first; General Anil Chauhan September 2022–May 2026; <b>General N.S. Raja Subramani</b> since 31 May 2026) is the <b>Permanent Chairman of the Chiefs of Staff Committee</b> (1), the <b>Principal Military Adviser to the Defence Minister</b> on tri-service matters (3), and <b>Secretary of the Department of Military Affairs (DMA)</b>. The CDS <b>does not exercise military command</b> over the three service chiefs, who continue to advise the Minister on their own services (2 is wrong).</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: CDS and defence reforms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recommended by the <b>Kargil Review Committee</b> (K. Subrahmanyam, 1999) and the Group of Ministers (2001), and later the Naresh Chandra Task Force (2012) and Shekatkar Committee (2016). Announced on Independence Day 2019.</li>
<li>Four-star general, paid like a service chief. Age limit 65. Not eligible for any government post after retirement (without permission).</li>
<li><b>Functions:</b> jointness in operations, logistics, training, procurement. Bring about <b>theatre commands</b>. Military adviser to the <b>Nuclear Command Authority</b>. Administer tri-service bodies (Andaman &amp; Nicobar Command, Strategic Forces Command, Defence Cyber Agency, Defence Space Agency, Armed Forces Special Operations Division).</li>
<li><b>Department of Military Affairs</b>: the 5th department in the Ministry of Defence (others: Defence, Defence Production, R&amp;D, Ex-Servicemen Welfare).</li>
<li><b>Inter-Services Organisations (Command, Control and Discipline) Act 2023</b> gives tri-service commanders disciplinary powers.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-78", year: 2024, no: 78, subject: "science", topic: "Defence: Army operations",
  q: `Operations undertaken by the Army towards upliftment of the local population in remote areas to include addressing of their basic needs is called:`,
  opts: ["Operation Sankalp", "Operation Maitri", "Operation Sadbhavana", "Operation Madad"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Operation Sadbhavana</h3>
<p><b>Operation Sadbhavana</b> ("goodwill") is the Indian Army's civic action programme, started in the late 1990s in <b>Jammu &amp; Kashmir</b> and extended to Ladakh and the Northeast. It funds schools (Army Goodwill Schools), healthcare, women's empowerment, infrastructure, skill training and educational tours in remote and conflict-affected areas, to win hearts and minds ("WHAM").</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Operation Sankalp: Indian Navy's maritime security deployment in the Gulf (2019).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Operation Maitri: relief to Nepal after the 2015 earthquake.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Operation Madad: Navy flood relief (e.g. Kerala 2018).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Other operations</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Operation</th><th>What</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sankalp</td><td>Indian Navy's maritime security operation in the Gulf of Oman and Persian Gulf (2019) to escort Indian ships</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maitri</td><td>Nepal earthquake relief (2015). (Also the name of India's Antarctic station.)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Madad</td><td>Navy's flood relief in Kerala (2018)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rahat</td><td>Evacuation from Yemen (2015); Uttarakhand flood relief (2013)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ganga</td><td>Evacuation from Ukraine (2022)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kaveri</td><td>Evacuation from Sudan (2023)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ajay</td><td>Evacuation from Israel (2023)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dost</td><td>Turkey–Syria earthquake relief (2023)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sindhu</td><td>Evacuation from Iran and Israel (2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brahma</td><td>Myanmar earthquake relief (2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sindoor</td><td>Strikes on terror camps in Pakistan and PoK (May 2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vijay</td><td>Goa liberation (1961); Kargil war (1999)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Meghdoot</td><td>Occupation of Siachen glacier (1984)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<h3>Latest updates (as of 2026)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>2026:</b> during the US–Israel–Iran war (from February 2026), India evacuated or helped return about 1.4 lakh Indians from Iran and the Gulf (MEA, July 2026).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-79", year: 2024, no: 79, subject: "geography", topic: "World: international borders",
  q: `The longest border between any two countries in the world is between:`,
  opts: ["Canada and the United States of America", "Chile and Argentina", "China and India", "Kazakhstan and Russian Federation"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Canada and the United States of America</h3>
<p>The <b>Canada–USA border</b> is about <b>8,891 km</b> long (including the Alaska–Canada border of about 2,475 km), the longest international border in the world. It runs largely along the <b>49th parallel</b> in the west and through the Great Lakes, and is famously undefended.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Chile–Argentina (about 5,300 km) is the third longest.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> China–India (about 3,488 km) is far shorter.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Kazakhstan–Russia (about 7,600 km) is the longest continuous land border but second overall.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Longest land borders</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Rank</th><th>Border</th><th>Length (approx.)</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Canada–USA</td><td>8,891 km</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Kazakhstan–Russia</td><td>7,644 km (longest continuous land border)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Argentina–Chile</td><td>5,300+ km (along the Andes)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>China–Mongolia</td><td>4,630 km</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>India–Bangladesh</td><td>4,096 km (India's longest)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Countries with the most neighbours: <b>China and Russia (14 each)</b>, Brazil (10).</li>
<li>India–China border is about 3,488 km: Western (Ladakh), Middle (Himachal, Uttarakhand), Eastern (Sikkim, Arunachal: McMahon Line). The LAC is undemarcated.</li>
<li>Famous lines: <b>Radcliffe Line</b> (India–Pakistan, India–Bangladesh), <b>Durand Line</b> (Afghanistan–Pakistan), <b>McMahon Line</b> (India–China), <b>38th parallel</b> (Korea), <b>49th parallel</b> (USA–Canada), <b>Mannerheim Line</b>.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-80", year: 2024, no: 80, subject: "polity", topic: "Parliament: Ethics Committee",
  q: `Which of the following statements about the Ethics Committee in the Lok Sabha are correct?
<ol><li>Initially it was an ad-hoc Committee.</li><li>Only a Member of the Lok Sabha can make a complaint relating to unethical conduct of a member of the Lok Sabha.</li><li>This Committee cannot take up any matter which is sub-judice.</li></ol>
Select the answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the Lok Sabha Ethics Committee was first constituted as an <b>ad hoc</b> committee by Speaker G.M.C. Balayogi in <b>2000</b>. It became a <b>permanent</b> part of the Rules in <b>2015</b>.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> any <b>person</b> (not only an MP) can complain, <b>through a Lok Sabha member</b>, with evidence and an affidavit that the complaint isn't false or vexatious. A member can complain directly. The committee can also take up matters <i>suo motu</i>.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> it does <b>not</b> take up matters that are <b>sub judice</b>, or based only on media reports.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Lok Sabha committee has <b>15 members</b>, appointed by the Speaker for one year. The <b>Rajya Sabha Ethics Committee</b> was set up earlier, in <b>1997</b> (Chairman K.R. Narayanan), with a Code of Conduct for members (2005).</li>
<li>History: Delhi presiding officers' conference (1996) recommended ethics panels. Cash-for-query case (2005: 11 MPs expelled, upheld in Raja Ram Pal, 2007). <b>Mahua Moitra</b> was expelled (December 2023) on the Ethics Committee's report on "cash for query", the first expulsion through the Ethics Committee route.</li>
<li>Other disciplinary routes: the <b>Committee of Privileges</b> (breach of privilege or contempt), the Speaker's powers of suspension (Rule 373, 374).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-81", year: 2024, no: 81, subject: "polity", topic: "Women's reservation",
  q: `Consider the following statements regarding 'Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam':
<ol><li>Provisions will come into effect from the 18th Lok Sabha.</li><li>This will be in force for 15 years after becoming an Act.</li><li>There are provisions for the reservation of seats for Scheduled Castes Women within the quota reserved for the Scheduled Castes.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<p>The <b>Constitution (106th Amendment) Act, 2023</b> (Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam) reserves <b>one-third of seats</b> for women in the <b>Lok Sabha</b>, <b>State Legislative Assemblies</b> and the <b>Delhi Legislative Assembly</b>.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> it takes effect only <b>after the first Census after the Act</b> and the <b>delimitation</b> based on it, so likely from the 2029 elections or later. Not the 18th Lok Sabha (elected in 2024).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> reservation lasts <b>15 years</b> from commencement, extendable by Parliament.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> one-third of the seats reserved for <b>SCs and STs</b> will be reserved for women of those groups (within the quota). There's no separate OBC sub-quota.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>New articles: <b>330A</b> (Lok Sabha), <b>332A</b> (Assemblies), <b>239AA(2)(ba)</b> (Delhi), <b>334A</b> (commencement and duration). Seats will <b>rotate</b> after each delimitation.</li>
<li>Does not cover the <b>Rajya Sabha</b> or <b>State Legislative Councils</b>.</li>
<li>Passed in the special session (September 2023) in the new Parliament building. Lok Sabha 454–2, Rajya Sabha unanimous. Earlier attempts: 81st Amendment Bill (1996, Deve Gowda), 108th Amendment Bill (passed by Rajya Sabha in 2010, lapsed).</li>
<li>Women MPs in the 18th Lok Sabha: 74 (13.6%). Panchayats already have one-third (many States 50%) under Art. 243D.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Latest updates (as of 2026)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The enabling Census is under way (house listing 2026, enumeration February 2027); reservation can start only after delimitation based on it.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-82", year: 2024, no: 82, subject: "ca", caYear: "2023", topic: "Military exercises",
  q: `Which of the following statements about 'Exercise Mitra Shakti-2023' are correct?
<ol><li>This was a joint military exercise between India and Bangladesh.</li><li>It commenced in Aundh (Pune).</li><li>Joint response during counter-terrorism operations was a goal of this operation.</li><li>Indian Air Force was a part of this exercise.</li></ol>
Select the answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1, 2 and 4", "1, 3 and 4", "2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2, 3 and 4</h3>
<p><b>Mitra Shakti</b> is the India–<b>Sri Lanka</b> joint military exercise, not India–Bangladesh (1 is wrong). The <b>9th edition (August 2023)</b> was held at <b>Aundh, Pune</b> (2), focused on <b>joint counter-terrorism operations</b> under a UN mandate (Chapter VII) (3), and included <b>Indian Air Force</b> participation alongside the Army (4).</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: India's bilateral exercises</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Country</th><th>Army</th><th>Navy</th><th>Air Force / joint</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sri Lanka</td><td><b>Mitra Shakti</b></td><td>SLINEX</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Bangladesh</td><td><b>Sampriti</b></td><td>Bongosagar</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Nepal</td><td>Surya Kiran</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>USA</td><td>Yudh Abhyas</td><td>Malabar (with Japan, Australia)</td><td>Cope India, Tarkash (NSG–SOF), Tiger Triumph (tri-service)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Russia</td><td></td><td>INDRA (tri-service)</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>France</td><td>Shakti</td><td>Varuna</td><td>Garuda</td></tr>
<tr><td>UK</td><td>Ajeya Warrior</td><td>Konkan</td><td>Indradhanush</td></tr>
<tr><td>Japan</td><td>Dharma Guardian</td><td>JIMEX</td><td>Veer Guardian</td></tr>
<tr><td>Australia</td><td>AustraHind</td><td>AUSINDEX</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Maldives</td><td>Ekuverin</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Mongolia</td><td>Nomadic Elephant</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Myanmar</td><td>IMBEX</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Singapore</td><td></td><td>SIMBEX</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Egypt</td><td>Cyclone</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Kazakhstan</td><td>Prabal Dostyk</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Multilateral: RIMPAC (US-led, Pacific), Pitch Black (Australia, air), Milan (India-hosted naval), Red Flag (USA, air), Tarang Shakti (India's first multinational air exercise, 2024).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-83", year: 2024, no: 83, subject: "polity", topic: "Judiciary: writs",
  q: `A Writ of Prohibition is an order issued by the Supreme Court or High Courts to:`,
  opts: ["a government officer prohibiting him from taking a particular action.", "the Parliament/Legislative Assembly to pass a law on Prohibition.", "the lower court prohibiting continuation of proceedings in a case.", "the Government prohibiting it from following an unconstitutional policy."], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the lower court prohibiting continuation of proceedings in a case</h3>
<p><b>Prohibition</b> ("to forbid") is issued by a higher court to a <b>lower court or tribunal</b> to stop it from continuing a case beyond its jurisdiction or in violation of natural justice. It is <b>preventive</b> (issued while proceedings are pending). It is not issued to administrative authorities, legislatures or private bodies.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Prohibition is issued only to judicial and quasi-judicial bodies, not to government officers (mandamus would apply to officers).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> No writ orders a legislature to pass a law.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Courts can strike down unconstitutional action through review or certiorari, but prohibition is not issued to the government.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: the five writs (Art. 32 SC, Art. 226 HC)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Writ</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Against</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Habeas corpus</b></td><td>"To have the body": produce a detained person and show lawful cause</td><td>Public authorities and private persons</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Mandamus</b></td><td>"We command": perform a public duty</td><td>Public officials, bodies, lower courts. Not against the President, Governors, private persons, or a private contract</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Prohibition</b></td><td>"To forbid": stop proceedings (preventive)</td><td>Judicial and quasi-judicial bodies only</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Certiorari</b></td><td>"To be certified": quash an order already passed (curative). Since 1991, also available against administrative authorities affecting rights</td><td>Judicial, quasi-judicial and administrative bodies</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Quo warranto</b></td><td>"By what authority": inquire into the legality of a claim to a public office</td><td>Holders of substantive public offices created by law. Any person can seek it</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Art. 32 is a <b>fundamental right</b> itself ("heart and soul of the Constitution": Ambedkar), only for FRs. Art. 226 is wider: for FRs "and for any other purpose" (legal rights too). High Courts' writ jurisdiction is part of basic structure (L. Chandra Kumar, 1997).</li>
<li>Parliament can empower other courts to issue writs (Art. 32(3)), but hasn't done so.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-84", year: 2024, no: 84, subject: "polity", topic: "Scheduled Tribes: notification",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>It is the Governor of the State who recognizes and declares any community of that State as a Scheduled Tribe.</li><li>A community declared as a Scheduled Tribe in a State need not be so in another State.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> under <b>Article 342(1)</b>, the <b>President</b>, after consulting the Governor, notifies the tribes or tribal communities deemed to be STs in each State or UT. Only <b>Parliament</b> can then include or exclude communities by law (Art. 342(2)).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> ST status is <b>State/UT-specific</b>. A community may be an ST in one State and not in another (e.g. the <b>Lambadas/Banjaras</b> are ST in Telangana but OBC or SC elsewhere; <b>Gonds</b> have different status across States). A migrant doesn't carry ST status to another State (Marri Chandra Shekhar Rao, 1990).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: STs</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Constitution doesn't define criteria. The <b>Lokur Committee (1965)</b> criteria: primitive traits, distinctive culture, geographical isolation, shyness of contact with the community at large, backwardness. Still used.</li>
<li>About 705 ST communities, 10.4 crore people (8.6%, Census 2011). Largest numbers: MP, Maharashtra, Odisha. Highest share: Mizoram, Lakshadweep, Meghalaya, Nagaland (above 85%). Punjab, Haryana, Delhi, Chandigarh, Puducherry have no STs.</li>
<li><b>PVTGs</b> (Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups): 75, across 18 States + A&amp;N. <b>PM-JANMAN</b> (2023, ₹24,000 crore) for PVTG habitations.</li>
<li>Process to include a community: State proposal → Registrar General of India → National Commission for STs → Cabinet → Parliament amends the Constitution (ST) Order 1950.</li>
<li>Same process for SCs under Art. 341. For SCs, only Hindus, Sikhs and Buddhists qualify (Constitution (SC) Order 1950). STs can be of any religion.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-85", year: 2024, no: 85, subject: "polity", topic: "Union Budget: procedure",
  q: `With reference to Union Budget, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Union Finance Minister on behalf of the Prime Minister lays the Annual Financial Statement before both the Houses of Parliament.</li><li>At the Union level, no demand for a grant can be made except on the recommendation of the President of India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2 (official key)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>Article 113(3)</b>: "No demand for a grant shall be made except on the recommendation of the President."</li>
<li><b>1 (accepted as correct by the key):</b> <b>Article 112</b> says the <b>President</b> "shall cause to be laid" the Annual Financial Statement before both Houses. In practice the <b>Finance Minister</b> presents it. UPSC's official key treats Statement 1 as correct.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Strictly, the Finance Minister presents the budget <b>on behalf of the President</b> (Art. 112), not the Prime Minister. Most analysts expected (b). UPSC's final key nevertheless says (c). For exams, remember the constitutional text: Art. 112, the President causes the AFS to be laid.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes: budget in the Constitution</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Constitution never uses the word "budget". It says <b>"Annual Financial Statement"</b> (Art. 112).</li>
<li><b>Stages:</b> presentation (1 February since 2017; Railway Budget merged in 2017) → general discussion → departmental standing committees scrutiny → voting on <b>demands for grants</b> (Lok Sabha only; <b>guillotine</b> on the last day) → <b>Appropriation Bill</b> (Art. 114) → <b>Finance Bill</b> (tax proposals, a Money Bill).</li>
<li><b>Cut motions:</b> policy cut (reduce to ₹1), economy cut (by a specific amount), token cut (by ₹100).</li>
<li><b>Vote on account</b> (Art. 116): interim grant for part of the year (usually 2 months; in election years, an interim budget).</li>
<li><b>Charged expenditure</b> (not voted, can be discussed): President's emoluments, salaries of SC judges, CAG, UPSC, Speaker/Chairman, debt charges, decrees of courts (Art. 112(3)).</li>
<li>Other grants: supplementary, additional, excess (needs PAC approval), exceptional, token grants.</li>
<li>Other budget documents: Budget at a Glance, Receipt Budget, Expenditure Budget, Statement on Fiscal Policy (FRBM), Outcome Budget, Gender Budget statement.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-86", year: 2024, no: 86, subject: "ca", caYear: "2024", topic: "Books and authors",
  q: `Who of the following is the author of the books "The India Way" and "Why Bharat Matters"?`,
  opts: ["Bhupender Yadav", "Nalin Mehta", "Shashi Tharoor", "Subrahmanyam Jaishankar"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Subrahmanyam Jaishankar</h3>
<p><b>Dr S. Jaishankar</b>, India's External Affairs Minister (since 2019) and former Foreign Secretary, wrote <b><i>The India Way: Strategies for an Uncertain World</i></b> (2020) and <b><i>Why Bharat Matters</i></b> (2024). The books set out India's foreign-policy thinking: strategic autonomy, multi-alignment, using the Mahabharata's lessons, "India First", neighbourhood first, and engaging all poles of a multipolar world.</p>
<h3>Other options and their books</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Bhupender Yadav</b> (Environment Minister): co-wrote <i>The Rise of the BJP</i> (with Ila Patnaik).</li>
<li><b>Nalin Mehta</b>: <i>The New BJP: Modi and the Making of the World's Largest Political Party</i>.</li>
<li><b>Shashi Tharoor</b>: <i>An Era of Darkness</i>, <i>Why I Am a Hindu</i>, <i>The Great Indian Novel</i>, <i>Pax Indica</i>, <i>The Battle of Belonging</i>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Books by Indian leaders (frequently asked)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Book</th><th>Author</th></tr>
<tr><td><i>Exam Warriors</i></td><td>Narendra Modi</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>The Discovery of India</i>, <i>Glimpses of World History</i></td><td>Jawaharlal Nehru</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>Wings of Fire</i>, <i>Ignited Minds</i></td><td>A.P.J. Abdul Kalam</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>The Dramatic Decade</i>, <i>The Turbulent Years</i></td><td>Pranab Mukherjee</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>Changing India</i></td><td>Manmohan Singh</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>India 2020</i></td><td>A.P.J. Abdul Kalam with Y.S. Rajan</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>The Insider</i></td><td>P.V. Narasimha Rao</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2024-87", year: 2024, no: 87, subject: "ca", caYear: "2023–24", topic: "Countries in news",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th></th><th>Country</th><th>Reason for being in the news</th></tr>
<tr><td>1.</td><td>Argentina</td><td>Worst economic crisis</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.</td><td>Sudan</td><td>War between the country's regular army and paramilitary forces</td></tr>
<tr><td>3.</td><td>Turkey</td><td>Rescinded its membership of NATO</td></tr></table></div>
How many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["Only one pair", "Only two pairs", "All three pairs", "None of the pairs"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Only two pairs</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Argentina (correct):</b> its worst economic crisis in decades: inflation above 200% (2023), over 40% poverty, recurring debt defaults. President <b>Javier Milei</b> (December 2023) pushed "chainsaw" austerity and devaluation. Inflation fell sharply in 2024–25, with a new IMF programme ($20 billion, 2025) and US support.</li>
<li><b>Sudan (correct):</b> civil war since <b>April 2023</b> between the <b>Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF)</b> under Gen. Abdel Fattah al-Burhan and the paramilitary <b>Rapid Support Forces (RSF)</b> under "Hemedti" (Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo). The world's largest displacement crisis (about 12+ million), famine, the fall of El Fasher (2025). India's <b>Operation Kaveri</b> evacuated Indians.</li>
<li><b>Turkey (wrong):</b> Turkey is still a <b>NATO member</b> (since 1952). It was in news for <b>delaying Sweden's accession</b> (ratified January 2024), not for leaving. NATO's treaty has a withdrawal clause (Article 13), but no member has ever left.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: crises in news (2023–25)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Russia–Ukraine war (since February 2022). Israel–Hamas war (October 2023), Gaza humanitarian crisis, ceasefire (October 2025). Israel–Iran 12-day war (June 2025).</li>
<li>Sahel coups: Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger left ECOWAS and formed the Alliance of Sahel States (2024).</li>
<li>Myanmar civil war after the 2021 coup. Haiti gang violence (Kenya-led security mission). Bangladesh political transition (August 2024). Nepal's Gen-Z protests (September 2025).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Latest updates (as of 2026)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>2026 Iran war:</b> US and Israel attacked Iran on 28 February 2026; Supreme Leader <b>Ali Khamenei was killed</b> and his son <b>Mojtaba Khamenei</b> succeeded him. Iran struck Israel and Gulf states hosting US bases and effectively shut the <b>Strait of Hormuz</b>; a Pakistan-mediated ceasefire (April 2026) was fragile and a US naval blockade continued. India brought home about 1.4 lakh nationals and faced costlier oil and LNG imports.</li><li><b>Venezuela:</b> US forces captured President Nicolás Maduro on 3 January 2026 and flew him to New York to face narco-terrorism charges.</li><li><b>Gaza:</b> phase two of the US-brokered ceasefire plan began in January 2026 (Trump-chaired "Board of Peace", Palestinian technocratic committee); Hamas disarmament and an international stabilisation force remained unresolved.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-88", year: 2024, no: 88, subject: "geography", topic: "Energy routes: SUMED",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> Sumed pipeline is a strategic route for Persian Gulf oil and natural gas shipments to Europe.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> Sumed pipeline connects the Red Sea with the Mediterranean Sea.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both correct and II explains I</h3>
<p>The <b>SUMED (Suez–Mediterranean) pipeline</b> in <b>Egypt</b> runs about 320 km from <b>Ain Sukhna</b> on the Gulf of Suez (<b>Red Sea</b>) to <b>Sidi Kerir</b> near Alexandria on the <b>Mediterranean</b>. Supertankers too large for the Suez Canal (when fully loaded) unload Gulf crude at Ain Sukhna. It is pumped to the Mediterranean, then re-loaded for Europe. It is a strategic alternative to the canal, which is why connecting the two seas makes it a key route to Europe.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (correct):</b> SUMED is a strategic route that moves Gulf crude toward Europe.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> It runs from Ain Sukhna (Red Sea) to Sidi Kerir (Mediterranean), which is why it is strategic; II explains I.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: key oil and gas pipelines</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Pipeline</th><th>Route</th></tr>
<tr><td>SUMED</td><td>Red Sea → Mediterranean (Egypt). Owned by Arab Petroleum Pipelines Co. (Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, UAE, Qatar)</td></tr>
<tr><td>East–West (Petroline)</td><td>Saudi Arabia: Abqaiq → Yanbu (Red Sea), bypassing Hormuz</td></tr>
<tr><td>Habshan–Fujairah (ADCOP)</td><td>UAE, bypasses Hormuz</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kirkuk–Ceyhan</td><td>Iraq → Turkey's Mediterranean coast</td></tr>
<tr><td>Druzhba</td><td>Russia → Europe (world's longest oil pipeline network)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nord Stream 1 &amp; 2</td><td>Russia → Germany under the Baltic (sabotaged in 2022)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TurkStream</td><td>Russia → Turkey → Europe under the Black Sea</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power of Siberia</td><td>Russia → China (gas)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TAPI (proposed)</td><td>Turkmenistan–Afghanistan–Pakistan–India gas</td></tr>
<tr><td>BTC</td><td>Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan (Azerbaijan crude to Turkey)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Red Sea shipping disruptions by the Houthis (2023–25) re-routed tankers around the Cape of Good Hope, raising SUMED's and Suez's importance.</p>
<h3>Latest updates (as of 2026)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>2026:</b> the US–Israel war with Iran (from 28 February 2026) nearly closed the <b>Strait of Hormuz</b> (about a fifth of world oil, and roughly 40–50% of India's crude imports and over half its LNG pass through it), sending Brent above $80 and raising the value of alternative routes such as SUMED, the East–West (Petroline) pipeline to Yanbu, and the Habshan–Fujairah pipeline.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-89", year: 2024, no: 89, subject: "geography", topic: "World: Red Sea",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Red Sea receives very little precipitation in any form.</li><li>No water enters the Red Sea from rivers.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<p>The <b>Red Sea</b> lies in one of the world's driest belts (between the Sahara/Nubian and Arabian deserts). It receives very little rain (about 60 mm a year) while <b>evaporation is very high</b> (about 2 m a year). <b>No permanent river flows into it</b>. It gets water only through the Bab-el-Mandeb strait from the Gulf of Aden. As a result it is one of the <b>saltiest seas</b> (about 36–41 ‰) and among the warmest.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> The Red Sea lies in a desert belt and gets only about 60 mm of rain a year.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> No permanent river flows into it; water enters only through Bab-el-Mandeb.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Red Sea</h3>
<ul>
<li>A young ocean basin in the <b>Afro-Arabian rift</b> (part of the East African Rift system), widening about 1–2 cm a year. It will become an ocean in the far future.</li>
<li>Bordering countries: Egypt, Sudan, Eritrea, Djibouti (Africa); Saudi Arabia, Yemen (Asia); also Israel and Jordan (Gulf of Aqaba).</li>
<li>Northern arms: <b>Gulf of Suez</b> (Suez Canal) and <b>Gulf of Aqaba</b> (Eilat, Aqaba). Southern exit: <b>Bab-el-Mandeb</b> ("gate of tears").</li>
<li>The name probably comes from seasonal blooms of red cyanobacteria (<i>Trichodesmium</i>).</li>
<li>Rich coral reefs, among the most heat-tolerant in the world.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Salinity comparison</h3>
<p>Average ocean about 35 ‰. Highest open-sea salinity: Red Sea and Persian Gulf (about 40 ‰). Dead Sea about 340 ‰. <b>Lake Van</b> (Turkey) about 330 ‰. Baltic Sea lowest (about 7–8 ‰) because of many rivers and low evaporation.</p>
<h3>Latest updates (as of 2026)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>2026:</b> the Houthis declared a naval blockade on Saudi Arabia (20 July 2026) and hit Saudi-linked tankers near Yanbu; Bab el-Mandeb traffic dipped, but Suez Canal transits in August 2026 were the highest since February 2024 (still about 35–40% below normal), per Lloyd's List.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-90", year: 2024, no: 90, subject: "environment", topic: "Air pollution: sulphur dioxide",
  q: `According to the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), which one of the following is the largest source of sulphur dioxide emissions?`,
  opts: ["Locomotives using fossil fuels", "Ships using fossil fuels", "Extraction of metals from ores", "Power plants using fossil fuels"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Power plants using fossil fuels</h3>
<p>The US EPA notes that the largest source of SO₂ in the atmosphere is the <b>burning of fossil fuels by power plants and other industrial facilities</b>, especially coal, which contains sulphur. Smaller sources include metal smelting (extracting metals from sulphide ores), locomotives, ships and other heavy equipment burning high-sulphur fuel, and natural sources like volcanoes.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Locomotives burning diesel are a minor SO2 source.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Ships using high-sulphur fuel are a notable but smaller source than power plants.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Metal smelting from sulphide ores is a significant but smaller source.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: SO₂</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Effects:</b> respiratory illness, asthma. Forms <b>sulphate aerosols</b> (fine particulate matter, PM2.5) and <b>acid rain</b> (sulphuric acid): damages forests, lakes, buildings (the "<b>marble cancer</b>" of the Taj Mahal, from Mathura refinery emissions; Taj Trapezium Zone).</li>
<li><b>India is the largest SO₂ emitter in the world</b> (mostly coal power plants), ahead of Russia and China, per NASA and Greenpeace analyses (2019).</li>
<li><b>Controls:</b> <b>flue gas desulphurisation (FGD)</b> scrubbers. MoEFCC set SO₂ limits for thermal power plants in 2015 with deadlines repeatedly extended. A 2025 notification exempted about 78% of plants (those away from dense cities and polluted areas) from FGD.</li>
<li><b>Shipping:</b> IMO 2020 cut the sulphur limit in marine fuel from 3.5% to 0.5%.</li>
<li>Fuel norms: <b>BS-VI</b> (April 2020) limits sulphur in petrol and diesel to 10 ppm.</li>
<li>SO₂ is one of the 8 pollutants in India's <b>National Air Quality Index</b> (PM10, PM2.5, NO₂, SO₂, CO, O₃, NH₃, Pb). <b>NCAP</b> (2019) targets a 40% reduction in PM by 2026 in 131 cities.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-91", year: 2024, no: 91, subject: "ca", caYear: "2023–24", topic: "Africa: Sahel",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> There is instability and worsening security situation in the Sahel region.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> There have been military takeovers/coups d'état in several countries of the Sahel region in the recent past.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both correct and II explains I</h3>
<p>The <b>Sahel</b> has seen a wave of coups: <b>Mali</b> (2020, 2021), <b>Burkina Faso</b> (two in 2022), <b>Niger</b> (2023), plus Chad (2021), Guinea (2021) and Sudan (2021). Juntas expelled French forces and UN peacekeepers (MINUSMA left Mali in 2023) and turned to Russia's Wagner/Africa Corps. Jihadist groups (JNIM linked to al-Qaeda, Islamic State Sahel) expanded. The coups and the security vacuum <b>explain</b> the worsening instability.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (correct):</b> The Sahel faces worsening insecurity with jihadist expansion.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> Coups in Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger and others weakened security cooperation, which explains I.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: the Sahel</h3>
<ul>
<li>"Sahel" = shore (Arabic): a semi-arid belt (about 200–600 mm rain) between the <b>Sahara</b> to the north and the <b>Sudanian savanna</b> to the south. Countries: Senegal, Mauritania, Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger, Nigeria (north), Chad, Sudan, Eritrea.</li>
<li>Problems: desertification, drought, population pressure, herder–farmer conflicts, climate change, weak states, jihadism. Great Green Wall initiative (African Union, 2007) to restore 100 million ha.</li>
<li>Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger formed the <b>Alliance of Sahel States (AES)</b> (2023 pact, confederation 2024) and <b>left ECOWAS</b> (January 2025).</li>
<li>Lake Chad Basin: Boko Haram, ISWAP.</li>
<li>India's interest: uranium in Niger, Africa outreach (India–Africa Forum Summit), IT and training via ITEC, the African Union's G20 membership (2023, during India's presidency).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-92", year: 2024, no: 92, subject: "economy", topic: "Trade: GM food & apples",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> India does not import apples from the United States of America.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> In India, the law prohibits the import of Genetically Modified food without the approval of the competent authority.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct</h3>
<p>India <b>does import</b> apples from the USA (Washington apples). In <b>2023</b> India removed the <b>retaliatory 20% duty</b> on US apples (imposed in 2019 over US steel and aluminium tariffs) as part of a WTO dispute settlement. Imports then rose sharply. Under the <b>Food Safety and Standards Act 2006</b> (Section 22), <b>GM food</b> can't be manufactured, sold or imported without <b>FSSAI</b>'s approval (and GEAC clearance for living modified organisms) (2). US apples are not GM, so II doesn't relate to I anyway.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (wrong):</b> India does import apples from the USA (Washington apples); the retaliatory duty was removed in 2023.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> GM food cannot be imported without approval of the competent authority (FSSAI, FSS Act Section 22).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: GM regulation in India</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC)</b> under MoEFCC (Rules 1989 under the Environment Protection Act 1986) approves environmental release of GM organisms.</li>
<li>Only GM crop approved for commercial cultivation: <b>Bt cotton</b> (2002). <b>Bt brinjal</b>: approved by GEAC (2009) but a moratorium was placed in 2010. <b>GM mustard (DMH-11)</b>: environmental release approved in 2022, stuck in the Supreme Court (split verdict July 2024, directed a national policy on GM crops). HT Bt cotton is illegally grown.</li>
<li><b>Genome-edited crops</b> (SDN-1, SDN-2, with no foreign DNA) were exempted from GM rules (2022). India released the world's first genome-edited rice varieties (DRR Dhan 100 Kamala, Pusa DST Rice 1) in May 2025.</li>
<li>FSSAI's 2021 order: GM-free certificate needed for imports of 24 food crops.</li>
<li>India–US trade: apples, walnuts, almonds, chickpeas, lentils (2023 tariff deal). US tariffs on India rose to 50% in August 2025 (25% reciprocal + 25% penalty over Russian oil); a February 2026 interim framework cut them to 18%, and after the US Supreme Court struck down IEEPA tariffs (February 2026) a temporary 10% global tariff under Section 122 applied.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-93", year: 2024, no: 93, subject: "polity", topic: "Parliament: Speaker removal",
  q: `With reference to the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, consider the following statements:
While any resolution for the removal of the Speaker of the Lok Sabha is under consideration
<ol><li>He/She shall not preside.</li><li>He/She shall not have the right to speak.</li><li>He/She shall not be entitled to vote on the resolution in the first instance.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<p><b>Article 96</b>: while a resolution for the Speaker's removal is under consideration, the Speaker <b>shall not preside</b> (1). But the Speaker <b>has the right to speak</b> and take part in the proceedings (2 is wrong), and <b>is entitled to vote in the first instance</b> on the resolution and other matters, though <b>not in case of an equality of votes</b> (no casting vote) (3 is wrong).</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Article 96: the Speaker shall not preside while a removal resolution is under consideration.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> The Speaker has the right to speak and take part in the proceedings.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The Speaker can vote in the first instance (but has no casting vote).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Speaker's removal</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Art. 94(c):</b> removal by a resolution passed by a <b>majority of all the then members</b> of the Lok Sabha (effective majority), after at least <b>14 days' notice</b>.</li>
<li>Same rules apply to the <b>Deputy Speaker</b>. For the <b>Rajya Sabha Chairman</b> (Vice-President): resolution by an effective majority of the Rajya Sabha agreed to by a simple majority of the Lok Sabha (Art. 67(b)), with 14 days' notice. While under consideration, the Chairman can't preside but can speak, and can't vote at all (Art. 92).</li>
<li>Other vacancies: resignation (to the Deputy Speaker), ceasing to be a member.</li>
<li>No Speaker has ever been removed. Motions were moved against G.V. Mavalankar (1951), Hukam Singh (1966) and Balram Jakhar (1987), and failed. In December 2024 the opposition gave notice to remove the Rajya Sabha Chairman (Jagdeep Dhankhar), which was rejected on technical grounds.</li>
<li>Asked in both 2024 and 2025: UPSC repeats the Speaker's office.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-94", year: 2024, no: 94, subject: "polity", topic: "Parliament: lapse of bills",
  q: `With reference to the Indian Parliament, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>A bill pending in the Lok Sabha lapses on its dissolution.</li><li>A bill passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha lapses on the dissolution of the Lok Sabha.</li><li>A bill in regard to which the President of India notified his/her intention to summon the Houses to a joint sitting lapses on the dissolution of the Lok Sabha.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2", "2 and 3", "3 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> any bill <b>pending in the Lok Sabha</b> (whether originating there or transmitted by the Rajya Sabha) lapses when the Lok Sabha is dissolved.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> a bill <b>passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha</b> lapses.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> a bill for which the President has notified a <b>joint sitting</b> before dissolution does <b>not</b> lapse (Art. 108(5)).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: bills that don't lapse on dissolution</h3>
<ol>
<li>A bill pending in the <b>Rajya Sabha</b> but <b>not passed by the Lok Sabha</b> (the Rajya Sabha is a permanent House).</li>
<li>A bill passed by both Houses and <b>pending the President's assent</b>.</li>
<li>A bill passed by both Houses but <b>returned by the President</b> for reconsideration.</li>
<li>A bill for which a <b>joint sitting</b> has been notified.</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Example: the Women's Reservation Bill, passed by the Rajya Sabha in 2010 and then pending in the Lok Sabha, lapsed when the 15th Lok Sabha was dissolved (2014). A bill pending in the Lok Sabha lapses whichever House it started in.</li>
<li><b>Joint sitting (Art. 108)</b>: presided by the Speaker. Not for Money Bills or constitutional amendments. Held only <b>three times</b>: Dowry Prohibition Bill (1961), Banking Service Commission (Repeal) Bill (1978), Prevention of Terrorism Bill (POTA, 2002).</li>
<li>Prorogation does <b>not</b> make bills lapse.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-95", year: 2024, no: 95, subject: "polity", topic: "Parliament: prorogation & dissolution",
  q: `With reference to the Parliament of India, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Prorogation of a House by the President of India does not require the advice of the Council of Ministers.</li><li>Prorogation of a House is generally done after the House is adjourned sine die but there is no bar to the President of India prorogating the House which is in session.</li><li>Dissolution of the Lok Sabha is done by the President of India who, save in exceptional circumstances, does so on the advice of the Council of Ministers.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2", "2 and 3", "3 only"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the President prorogues a House under <b>Article 85(2)(a)</b> on the <b>advice of the Council of Ministers</b> (Art. 74), like nearly all presidential powers.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> normally the presiding officer <b>adjourns the House sine die</b> at the end of a session, and the President then prorogues it within a few days. But the President can prorogue a House even while it is sitting.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> dissolution (Art. 85(2)(b)) is done by the President on the CoM's advice, except in exceptional situations (e.g. a PM who has lost majority advising dissolution, where the President may explore alternatives).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: ending a sitting or session</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th></th><th>Adjournment</th><th>Adjournment sine die</th><th>Prorogation</th><th>Dissolution</th></tr>
<tr><td>By</td><td>Presiding officer</td><td>Presiding officer</td><td>President</td><td>President (Lok Sabha only)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effect</td><td>Suspends a sitting for hours or days</td><td>Ends a sitting without a date to reconvene</td><td>Ends a <b>session</b></td><td>Ends the life of the Lok Sabha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pending bills</td><td>No effect</td><td>No effect</td><td>No effect (notices other than for bills lapse)</td><td>Some lapse (see Q94)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Sessions: <b>maximum gap of 6 months</b> between two sessions (Art. 85(1)). Usually three: Budget, Monsoon, Winter.</li>
<li>Quorum: one-tenth of total members (Art. 100(3)).</li>
<li>Lok Sabha term: 5 years, extendable by a year at a time during a national emergency (Art. 83(2)). Was extended once (1976).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-96", year: 2024, no: 96, subject: "ca", caYear: "2024", topic: "EU climate policy",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> The European Parliament approved The Net-Zero Industry Act recently.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> The European Union intends to achieve carbon neutrality by 2040 and therefore aims to develop all of its own clean technology by that time.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect</h3>
<p>The European Parliament approved the <b>Net-Zero Industry Act (NZIA)</b> in <b>April 2024</b> (in force June 2024). It aims for the EU to manufacture at least <b>40% of its annual clean-tech deployment needs by 2030</b> (solar PV, wind, batteries, heat pumps, electrolysers, CCS...). The EU's legally binding climate-neutrality target is <b>2050</b> (European Climate Law, 2021), with a 55% cut by 2030 ("Fit for 55"). It doesn't aim to make <i>all</i> its clean tech itself. (In 2025 the Commission proposed a <b>90% cut by 2040</b> as an interim target, not neutrality.)</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (correct):</b> The European Parliament approved the Net-Zero Industry Act in April 2024.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (wrong):</b> The EU's climate-neutrality target is 2050, and NZIA aims to make 40% of clean-tech needs by 2030, not all of it.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: EU Green Deal laws</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Law</th><th>Aim</th></tr>
<tr><td>European Climate Law (2021)</td><td>Climate neutrality by 2050, −55% by 2030</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fit for 55 package</td><td>ETS reform, ETS2 (buildings and transport), CBAM, ban on new petrol/diesel cars from 2035, Renewable Energy Directive (42.5% by 2030)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Net-Zero Industry Act</b> (2024)</td><td>40% domestic clean-tech manufacturing by 2030. Response to the US Inflation Reduction Act and Chinese dominance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Critical Raw Materials Act (2024)</td><td>10% extraction, 40% processing, 25% recycling domestically by 2030. No more than 65% from one country</td></tr>
<tr><td>CBAM</td><td>Carbon border levy (definitive from 2026)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nature Restoration Law (2024)</td><td>Restore 20% of land and sea by 2030</td></tr>
<tr><td>EUDR</td><td>Deforestation-free supply chains</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2024-97", year: 2024, no: 97, subject: "ca", caYear: "2023–24", topic: "Latin America: Venezuela",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<p><b>Statement-I:</b> Recently, Venezuela has achieved a rapid recovery from its economic crisis and succeeded in preventing its people from fleeing/emigrating to other countries.</p>
<p><b>Statement-II:</b> Venezuela has the world's largest oil reserves.</p>
Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?`,
  opts: ["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I", "Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I", "Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect", "Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct</h3>
<p><b>Venezuela</b> has the <b>world's largest proven oil reserves</b> (about 303 billion barrels, about 17% of the world's, mostly extra-heavy oil in the <b>Orinoco Belt</b>), ahead of Saudi Arabia. But it suffered one of the deepest economic collapses outside wartime (GDP shrank about 75% from 2014 to 2021, hyperinflation, shortages), and about <b>7.7–8 million</b> people (about a quarter of the population) have <b>emigrated</b>, the largest displacement crisis in Latin America. There was no "rapid recovery".</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement I (wrong):</b> Venezuela suffered a deep collapse and about 7.7–8 million people emigrated; there was no rapid recovery.</li>
<li><b>Statement II (correct):</b> Venezuela has the world's largest proven oil reserves (about 303 billion barrels).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Causes: dependence on oil (about 95% of exports), the 2014 oil price crash, mismanagement and nationalisation under Chávez (1999–2013) and Maduro, US sanctions (from 2017–19).</li>
<li>Politics: disputed 2024 presidential election (Maduro declared winner, opposition's Edmundo González claimed victory). Opposition leader <b>María Corina Machado</b> won the <b>2025 Nobel Peace Prize</b>. US military pressure in the Caribbean (2025), then a US strike and raid that <b>captured Maduro on 3 January 2026</b>; he faces trial in New York.</li>
<li><b>Largest proven oil reserves:</b> Venezuela, Saudi Arabia, Iran, Canada (oil sands), Iraq. Largest producers: USA, Saudi Arabia, Russia. India imports crude mainly from Russia, Iraq, Saudi Arabia, UAE, USA.</li>
<li>Largest natural gas reserves: Russia, Iran, Qatar.</li>
<li>OPEC (1960, Baghdad; HQ Vienna). Venezuela is a founding member. OPEC+ includes Russia.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-98", year: 2024, no: 98, subject: "polity", topic: "Governance: land records",
  q: `With reference to the Digital India Land Records Modernisation Programme, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>To implement the scheme, the Central Government provides 100% funding.</li><li>Under the Scheme, Cadastral Maps are digitised.</li><li>An initiative has been undertaken to transliterate the Records of Rights from local language to any of the languages recognized by the Constitution of India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<p>The <b>Digital India Land Records Modernisation Programme (DILRMP)</b> became a <b>Central Sector Scheme with 100% central funding</b> from 1 April 2016 (1). It covers <b>digitisation of cadastral maps</b>, computerisation of Records of Rights (RoR), registration, survey/resurvey, and integration of land records with registration (2). An initiative is underway to <b>transliterate RoRs into any of the 22 Eighth Schedule languages</b> to overcome language barriers (3). Extended to 2025–26.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: land records reforms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Origin: merger (2008) of Computerisation of Land Records (CLR) and Strengthening of Revenue Administration and Updating of Land Records (SRA&amp;ULR) into NLRMP, renamed DILRMP. Department of Land Resources, Ministry of Rural Development.</li>
<li><b>ULPIN</b> (Unique Land Parcel Identification Number, "Bhu-Aadhaar"): a 14-digit alphanumeric ID for each plot, based on geo-coordinates.</li>
<li><b>NGDRS</b> (National Generic Document Registration System): online deed registration.</li>
<li><b>SVAMITVA</b> (2020): drone survey of rural inhabited areas and property cards (Ministry of Panchayati Raj).</li>
<li>Goal: <b>conclusive titling</b> (state-guaranteed titles) instead of presumptive titles. NITI Aayog's Model Conclusive Land Titling Act. Rajasthan's Urban Land (Certification of Titles) Act 2016.</li>
<li>Budget 2024–25: Bhu-Aadhaar for all rural land and digitisation of urban land records (NAKSHA programme, 2025).</li>
<li>Land is a State subject (Entry 18, List II).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-99", year: 2024, no: 99, subject: "polity", topic: "Health schemes: maternal care",
  q: `With reference to the 'Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan', consider the following statements:
<ol><li>This scheme guarantees a minimum package of antenatal care services to women in their second and third trimesters of pregnancy and six months post-delivery health care service in any government health facility.</li><li>Under this scheme, private sector health care providers of certain specialities can volunteer to provide services at nearby government health facilities.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<p>The <b>Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA)</b> (launched <b>2016</b>, Ministry of Health) provides <b>assured, comprehensive, free antenatal check-ups</b> to pregnant women in their <b>2nd and 3rd trimesters</b> on the <b>9th of every month</b> at government health facilities. It does <b>not</b> cover six months of post-delivery care (1 is wrong). It invites <b>private OBGYN specialists and doctors to volunteer</b> at government facilities (2 is correct).</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: maternal and child health schemes</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Key feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY, 2005)</td><td>Cash incentive for institutional delivery (NHM)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK, 2011)</td><td>Free delivery, C-section, drugs, transport, diet in public facilities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY, 2017)</td><td>₹5,000 maternity benefit for the first child (+₹6,000 for a second girl child since 2022), under Mission Shakti. Implements the NFSA 2013 entitlement</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>PMSMA</b> (2016)</td><td>Free ANC on the 9th of each month. Extended PMSMA (2022) for high-risk pregnancies</td></tr>
<tr><td>LaQshya (2017)</td><td>Quality of labour rooms and maternity OTs</td></tr>
<tr><td>SUMAN (2019)</td><td>Zero tolerance for denial of maternal services</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mission Indradhanush / U-WIN</td><td>Immunisation</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Maternal Mortality Ratio</b>: 93 per 1 lakh live births (SRS 2019–21), down from 130 (2014–16). SDG target: below 70 by 2030. Kerala lowest, Assam highest.</li>
<li>Infant Mortality Rate has fallen below 30 per 1,000 live births (Sample Registration System).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2024-100", year: 2024, no: 100, subject: "polity", topic: "Social security: unorganised workers",
  q: `With reference to the Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan-dhan (PM-SYM) Yojana, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The entry age group for enrolment in the scheme is 21 to 40 years.</li><li>Age specific contribution shall be made by the beneficiary.</li><li>Each subscriber under the scheme shall receive a minimum pension of ₹3,000 per month after attaining the age of 60 years.</li><li>Family pension is applicable to the spouse and unmarried daughters.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 3 and 4", "2 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 4"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<p><b>PM-SYM</b> (February 2019, Ministry of Labour &amp; Employment) is a voluntary, contributory pension scheme for <b>unorganised workers</b> earning up to <b>₹15,000 a month</b> (street vendors, rickshaw pullers, domestic workers, agricultural labourers...).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> entry age is <b>18 to 40</b> years.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the worker pays an <b>age-specific monthly contribution</b> (₹55 at 18 up to ₹200 at 40), and the <b>Centre matches it equally</b> (50:50).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> <b>assured minimum pension of ₹3,000 a month</b> from age 60.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> family pension (50% of the pension) goes to the <b>spouse only</b>, not to children.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: pension and social security schemes</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>For</th><th>Key feature</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>PM-SYM</b> (2019)</td><td>Unorganised workers, 18–40, income ≤ ₹15,000/month</td><td>₹3,000 pension, 50:50 matching. LIC is the fund manager. Enrolment via CSCs</td></tr>
<tr><td>PM Kisan Maan-dhan (2019)</td><td>Small and marginal farmers, 18–40</td><td>₹3,000 pension at 60</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Pension Scheme for Traders and Self-employed (2019)</td><td>Traders with turnover ≤ ₹1.5 crore</td><td>₹3,000 pension (merged with PM-SYM)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atal Pension Yojana (2015)</td><td>18–40, non-taxpayers (since 2022)</td><td>₹1,000–5,000 pension. PFRDA</td></tr>
<tr><td>NPS (2004)</td><td>Government employees (new), all citizens</td><td>Market-linked. <b>Unified Pension Scheme</b> (from April 2025) assures 50% of last pay for central employees opting in</td></tr>
<tr><td>e-Shram portal (2021)</td><td>Registration of unorganised workers</td><td>About 30+ crore registered. Code on Social Security 2020 (in force November 2025) covers gig and platform workers</td></tr>
</table></div>` }

);
;
