window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2019-76", year: 2019, no: 76, subject: "polity", topic: "Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017",
  q: `Which of the following statements is/are correct regarding the Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017?
<ol><li>Pregnant women are entitled for three months pre-delivery and three months post-delivery paid leave.</li><li>Enterprises with crèches must allow the mother minimum six crèche visits daily.</li><li>Women with two children get reduced entitlements.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> paid maternity leave was raised from 12 to <b>26 weeks</b>, of which <b>not more than 8 weeks</b> can be taken before delivery.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> establishments with <b>50 or more employees</b> must have a crèche, and the woman is allowed <b>four</b> visits a day (including rest intervals).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> women with <b>two or more surviving children</b> get only <b>12 weeks</b> (6 weeks before delivery).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: other provisions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>12 weeks</b> for a mother adopting a child below 3 months and for a commissioning mother (surrogacy), from the date the child is handed over.</li>
<li><b>Work from home</b> may be allowed after maternity leave if the nature of work permits.</li>
<li>Employer must inform women in writing and electronically of benefits at the time of appointment.</li>
<li>Applies to establishments with 10 or more employees. Now part of the <b>Code on Social Security, 2020</b>.</li>
<li>Schemes: <b>PM Matru Vandana Yojana</b> (₹5,000 for the first child; ₹6,000 for a second child if a girl, under Mission Shakti 2022).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-77", year: 2019, no: 77, subject: "economy", topic: "Ease of Doing Business",
  q: `Which one of the following is not a sub-index of the World Bank's 'Ease of Doing Business Index'?`,
  opts: ["Maintenance of law and order", "Paying taxes", "Registering property", "Dealing with construction permits"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Maintenance of law and order</h3>
<p>The <b>Doing Business</b> report measured 10 indicators: <b>starting a business, dealing with construction permits, getting electricity, registering property, getting credit, protecting minority investors, paying taxes, trading across borders, enforcing contracts, resolving insolvency</b>. Law and order was not one of them.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India rose from 142nd (2015) to <b>63rd</b> (Doing Business 2020).</li>
<li>The World Bank <b>discontinued</b> the report in September 2021 after data irregularities (China, Saudi Arabia, UAE, Azerbaijan).</li>
<li>Replacement: <b>Business Ready (B-READY)</b>, first edition October 2024 (50 economies; India to be included later), with 10 topics across regulatory framework, public services and operational efficiency.</li>
<li>India's own: <b>Business Reform Action Plan (BRAP)</b> ranking of States by DPIIT; Jan Vishwas Act 2023 decriminalised 183 provisions; Jan Vishwas 2.0 Bill (2025).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-78", year: 2019, no: 78, subject: "environment", topic: "Extended Producer Responsibility",
  q: `In India, 'extended producer responsibility' was introduced as an important feature in which of the following?`,
  opts: ["The Bio-medical Waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 1998", "The Recycled Plastic (Manufacturing and Usage) Rules, 1999", "The e-Waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011", "The Food Safety and Standard Regulations, 2011"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) The e-Waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011</h3>
<p><b>Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR)</b> makes producers responsible for the environmentally sound management of their products at the end of life (take-back, collection, recycling). It was introduced as a central feature in India's <b>e-Waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011</b> (in force 2012).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The 1998 Bio-medical Waste Rules did not feature EPR.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The 1999 recycled plastic rules dealt with carry bag standards; EPR came for plastics in 2011/2016.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Food safety regulations do not involve EPR.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: EPR in Indian rules</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Rules</th><th>EPR feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>E-Waste Rules 2016, 2022</td><td>Collection targets; tradable EPR certificates on CPCB portal (2022); minimum price for EPR certificates (2024)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plastic Waste Management Rules 2016 (EPR guidelines 2022)</td><td>Targets for collection, recycling, use of recycled content for brand owners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Battery Waste Management Rules 2022</td><td>EPR for all battery types</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waste tyres (2022), used oil (2023)</td><td>EPR regimes</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWM Rules 2016</td><td>EPR for packaging, sanitary products</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>The concept was first developed in Sweden (Thomas Lindhqvist, 1990).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-79", year: 2019, no: 79, subject: "economy", topic: "Economic cost of foodgrains",
  q: `The economic cost of food grains to the Food Corporation of India is Minimum Support Price and bonus (if any) paid to the farmers plus`,
  opts: ["transportation cost only", "interest cost only", "procurement incidentals and distribution cost", "procurement incidentals and charges for godowns"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) procurement incidentals and distribution cost</h3>
<p>FCI's <b>economic cost</b> = <b>acquisition cost</b> (MSP + bonus + procurement incidentals) + <b>distribution cost</b>.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Procurement incidentals</b>: mandi charges, taxes and cess, commission to arhatiyas, gunny bags, labour, storage and interest during procurement, administrative charges.</li>
<li><b>Distribution cost</b>: freight, handling, storage, transit losses, interest on stocks till issue.</li>
</ul>
<p>Economic cost of rice about ₹39–40 per kg and wheat about ₹27–28 per kg (2024–25). The difference between economic cost and the Central Issue Price (zero under PMGKAY) is the <b>food subsidy</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Transportation alone is incomplete; storage, handling and interest are also included.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Interest alone is incomplete.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>FCI is funded partly by <b>National Small Savings Fund</b> loans (shown off-budget until 2020–21; moved on-budget in Budget 2021).</li>
<li>Decentralised Procurement (DCP, 1997): States procure and distribute; Centre reimburses.</li>
<li>Shanta Kumar Committee (2015): restructure FCI, cut NFSA coverage to 40%, cash transfers, negotiable warehouse receipts.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-80", year: 2019, no: 80, subject: "economy", topic: "Social capital",
  q: `In the context of any country, which one of the following would be considered as part of its social capital?`,
  opts: ["The proportion of literates in the population", "The stock of its buildings, other infrastructure and machines", "The size of population in the working age group", "The level of mutual trust and harmony in the society"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) The level of mutual trust and harmony in the society</h3>
<p><b>Social capital</b> means networks, norms, mutual <b>trust</b> and cooperation among people that make collective action easier (Robert Putnam, James Coleman, Pierre Bourdieu). High-trust societies have lower transaction costs and better governance.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> literacy is part of <b>human capital</b> (skills, education, health).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> buildings and machines are <b>physical capital</b>.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> working-age population relates to the <b>demographic dividend</b> (human resources).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: forms of capital</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Capital</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Physical</td><td>Factories, roads, machines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Human</td><td>Education, health, skills</td></tr>
<tr><td>Natural</td><td>Forests, minerals, water</td></tr>
<tr><td>Social</td><td>Trust, networks, institutions like SHGs, cooperatives</td></tr>
<tr><td>Financial</td><td>Money, credit</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Examples of social capital in India: SHG movement (Kudumbashree, DAY-NRLM), dairy cooperatives (Amul), Water User Associations.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-81", year: 2019, no: 81, subject: "polity", topic: "Judicial review powers",
  q: `With reference to the Constitution of India, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>No High Court shall have the jurisdiction to declare any central law to be constitutionally invalid.</li><li>An amendment to the Constitution of India cannot be called into question by the Supreme Court of India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> High Courts can declare central laws unconstitutional under <b>Article 226</b>. The <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b> had added Article 226A barring HCs from examining central laws, but the <b>43rd Amendment (1977)</b> repealed it. <b>L. Chandra Kumar (1997)</b> held judicial review by HCs and the SC to be part of the basic structure.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> amendments can be struck down if they violate the <b>basic structure</b> (Kesavananda Bharati 1973; Minerva Mills 1980; NJAC case 2015 struck down the 99th Amendment).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: amendment and judicial review</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Case</th><th>Holding</th></tr>
<tr><td>Shankari Prasad (1951), Sajjan Singh (1965)</td><td>Parliament can amend FRs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Golaknath (1967)</td><td>Parliament cannot amend FRs</td></tr>
<tr><td>24th Amendment (1971)</td><td>Restored amending power over FRs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kesavananda Bharati (1973)</td><td>Can amend any part, but not the basic structure</td></tr>
<tr><td>42nd Amendment (1976)</td><td>Art 368(4), (5): amendments immune from courts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minerva Mills (1980)</td><td>Struck down Art 368(4), (5); limited amending power is basic structure</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2019-82", year: 2019, no: 82, subject: "economy", topic: "Purchasing Power Parity",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Purchasing Power Parity (PPP) exchange rates are calculated by comparing the prices of the same basket of goods and services in different countries.</li><li>In terms of PPP dollars, India is the sixth largest economy in the world.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> PPP exchange rates equalise purchasing power by comparing the cost of a common basket of goods and services across countries. The World Bank's <b>International Comparison Program</b> (ICP) computes them (latest benchmark 2021, released 2024).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> by PPP, India is the <b>third-largest</b> economy (after China and the USA). By market exchange rates, India was sixth in 2019; it became fifth (2022); the IMF (October 2026) projects it to become fourth, passing Japan, in 2027.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Since goods and services are cheaper in India, India's GDP at PPP (about $17 trillion, 2025) is roughly four times its nominal GDP (about $4.2 trillion).</li>
<li><b>Big Mac Index</b> (The Economist): an informal PPP measure.</li>
<li>PPP is used for international poverty lines (World Bank $3 per day, 2021 PPP) and comparing living standards.</li>
<li>Nominal GDP ranking (2026, IMF October 2026 WEO): USA, China, Germany, Japan, India; India projected 4th in 2027 (Japan falling to 6th behind the UK).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Latest updates (as of 2026)</h3>
<ul>
<li>IMF (October 2026) raised India's 2026 growth forecast to 7.0%; Japan about 0.7%.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-83", year: 2019, no: 83, subject: "geography", topic: "Kharif crop area",
  q: `With reference to the cultivation of Kharif crops in India in the last five years, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Area under rice cultivation is the highest.</li><li>Area under the cultivation of jowar is more than that of oilseeds.</li><li>Area of cotton cultivation is more than that of sugarcane.</li><li>Area under sugarcane cultivation has steadily decreased.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> kharif rice covers about <b>40 million hectares</b>, the largest kharif crop area.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> kharif oilseeds (soybean, groundnut, sesamum, castor, about 18–19 Mha) far exceed kharif jowar (about 2–3 Mha).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> cotton (about 12–13 Mha) is far larger than sugarcane (about 5 Mha).</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> sugarcane area fluctuates with prices and rainfall; it has not steadily decreased.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: crop seasons</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Season</th><th>Sowing / harvest</th><th>Crops</th></tr>
<tr><td>Kharif</td><td>June–July / September–October</td><td>Rice, maize, jowar, bajra, ragi, tur, moong, urad, soybean, groundnut, cotton, jute</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rabi</td><td>October–December / March–April</td><td>Wheat, barley, gram, mustard, masoor, peas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zaid</td><td>March–June</td><td>Watermelon, cucumber, moong, fodder</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2019-84", year: 2019, no: 84, subject: "economy", topic: "Agricultural imports",
  q: `Among the agricultural commodities imported by India, which one of the following accounts for the highest imports in terms of value in the last five years?`,
  opts: ["Spices", "Fresh fruits", "Pulses", "Vegetable oils"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Vegetable oils</h3>
<p><b>Edible (vegetable) oils</b> are India's largest agricultural import by value, about <b>$15–20 billion</b> a year (14–16 million tonnes). India imports about 55–60% of its edible oil needs: <b>palm oil</b> (Indonesia, Malaysia), <b>soybean oil</b> (Argentina, Brazil), <b>sunflower oil</b> (Ukraine, Russia).</p>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other big agricultural imports: pulses (about $3–5 billion), fresh fruits, cashew (raw), spices, cotton.</li>
<li>Self-reliance efforts: <b>NMEO-Oil Palm</b> (2021), <b>NMEO-Oilseeds</b> (2024, ₹10,103 crore, target 69.7 MT oilseeds by 2030–31), Yellow Revolution (1986, Technology Mission on Oilseeds).</li>
<li>Import duties on edible oils are adjusted often to balance farmers' and consumers' interests.</li>
<li>India's agricultural exports (about $50 billion): rice, marine products, spices, sugar, buffalo meat.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-85", year: 2019, no: 85, subject: "polity", topic: "Meaning of liberty",
  q: `In the context of polity, which one of the following would you accept as the most appropriate definition of liberty?`,
  opts: ["Protection against the tyranny of political rulers", "Absence of restraint", "Opportunity to do whatever one likes", "Opportunity to develop oneself fully"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Opportunity to develop oneself fully</h3>
<p>The <b>positive</b> concept of liberty (T.H. Green, Harold Laski) sees liberty as the <b>presence of conditions/opportunities</b> in which people can develop their personality fully. Laski: "Liberty is the eager maintenance of that atmosphere in which men have the opportunity to be their best selves."</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> protection against tyranny is part of liberty but too narrow.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> "absence of restraint" is <b>negative liberty</b> (Hobbes, Bentham, Isaiah Berlin's description); incomplete since reasonable restraints protect others' liberty.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> doing whatever one likes is licence, not liberty.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: liberty in the Constitution</h3>
<ul>
<li>Preamble: liberty of <b>thought, expression, belief, faith and worship</b>.</li>
<li>Articles 19–22 (freedoms), 25–28 (religion). Reasonable restrictions keep liberty compatible with order.</li>
<li>Isaiah Berlin: "Two Concepts of Liberty" (1958), negative ("freedom from") vs positive ("freedom to"). J.S. Mill: "On Liberty" (1859), harm principle.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-86", year: 2019, no: 86, subject: "economy", topic: "Defending the rupee",
  q: `Which one of the following is not the most likely measure the Government/RBI takes to stop the slide of Indian rupee?`,
  opts: ["Curbing imports of non-essential goods and promoting exports", "Encouraging Indian borrowers to issue rupee denominated Masala Bonds", "Easing conditions relating to external commercial borrowing", "Following an expansionary monetary policy"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Following an expansionary monetary policy</h3>
<p>Expansionary policy (rate cuts, more liquidity) lowers interest rates, so foreign capital leaves and the rupee weakens further. To defend the rupee, the RBI usually <b>tightens</b>: raises rates, sells dollars from reserves.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> curbing imports (e.g. higher gold import duty) and promoting exports reduce dollar demand and raise supply.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Masala bonds raise foreign money without currency risk for the borrower (the investor bears it), bringing in dollars.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> easier ECB rules bring in foreign currency.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: rupee defence measures (2013, 2018, 2022)</h3>
<ul>
<li>2013 taper tantrum: FCNR(B) deposit swap window raised about $34 billion; gold import curbs (80:20 rule).</li>
<li>2022: higher ECB limit ($1.5 billion automatic), FPI debt limits eased, incremental FCNR(B)/NRE deposits exempted from CRR/SLR, rupee trade settlement (Special Rupee Vostro Accounts, July 2022).</li>
<li>Forex intervention: spot, forwards, NDF market.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-87", year: 2019, no: 87, subject: "economy", topic: "Payment data localisation",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
The Reserve Bank of India's recent directives relating to 'Storage of Payment System Data', popularly known as data diktat, command the payment system providers that
<ol><li>they shall ensure that entire data relating to payment systems operated by them are stored in a system only in India</li><li>they shall ensure that the systems are owned and operated by public sector enterprises</li><li>they shall submit the consolidated system audit report to the Comptroller and Auditor General of India by the end of the calendar year</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<p>The RBI's <b>April 2018</b> circular on Storage of Payment System Data required all payment system operators (Visa, Mastercard, PayPal, Google Pay, WhatsApp Pay etc.) to store the <b>entire data</b> (end-to-end transaction details) <b>only in India</b>, within six months (by 15 October 2018). For cross-border transactions, a foreign copy can be kept.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> no requirement of public-sector ownership.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> the system audit report (by a CERT-In empanelled auditor) is submitted to the <b>RBI</b>, not the CAG.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> All payment data must be stored only in India (April 2018 circular).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> There is no requirement of public sector ownership.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The audit report goes to the RBI, not the CAG.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The RBI barred <b>Mastercard</b> (July 2021), American Express and Diners Club from adding new customers for non-compliance; lifted later after compliance.</li>
<li>The <b>Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023</b> allows cross-border data transfer except to countries the government restricts (negative list), but sectoral rules like the RBI's still apply.</li>
<li>Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007: RBI's Board for Regulation and Supervision of Payment and Settlement Systems (BPSS).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-88", year: 2019, no: 88, subject: "ir", topic: "GDPR",
  q: `Which of the following adopted a law on data protection and privacy for its citizens known as 'General Data Protection Regulation' in April 2016 and started implementation of it from 25th May, 2018?`,
  opts: ["Australia", "Canada", "The European Union", "The United States of America"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) The European Union</h3>
<p>The EU's <b>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)</b> was adopted in April 2016 and applied from <b>25 May 2018</b>. It covers any organisation processing personal data of people in the EU, wherever the organisation is located.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Australia has the Privacy Act, 1988; not GDPR.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Canada has PIPEDA (2000); not GDPR.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The USA has no comprehensive federal data law; California has CCPA (2018).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: GDPR key features</h3>
<ul>
<li>Lawful basis and explicit <b>consent</b>; rights to access, rectification, erasure (<b>right to be forgotten</b>), data portability, objection.</li>
<li><b>Data breach notification within 72 hours</b>; Data Protection Officers; privacy by design.</li>
<li>Fines up to <b>€20 million or 4% of global annual turnover</b> (Meta fined €1.2 billion in 2023).</li>
<li>EU also passed the Digital Services Act, Digital Markets Act (2022) and <b>AI Act</b> (2024, first comprehensive AI law).</li>
</ul>
<h3>India's data protection</h3>
<ul>
<li>Justice <b>B.N. Srikrishna Committee</b> (2018) draft → PDP Bill 2019 (withdrawn 2022) → <b>Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023</b>: Data Fiduciaries, Data Principals, Data Protection Board, penalties up to ₹250 crore. DPDP Rules notified November 2025, phased implementation.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-89", year: 2019, no: 89, subject: "ir", topic: "India–Russia nuclear cooperation",
  q: `Recently, India signed a deal known as 'Action Plan for Prioritization and Implementation of Cooperation Areas in the Nuclear Field' with which of the following countries?`,
  opts: ["Japan", "Russia", "The United Kingdom", "The United States of America"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Russia</h3>
<p>India and <b>Russia</b> signed the "Action Plan for Prioritization and Implementation of Cooperation Areas in the Nuclear Field" during President Putin's visit in <b>October 2018</b> (19th annual summit). It covers new nuclear units, a second site for Russian-designed reactors, nuclear fuel, and joint projects in third countries (e.g. Rooppur in Bangladesh).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(c)</b> India and the UK have a civil nuclear agreement (2015) but not this action plan.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The India-US deal is the 123 Agreement (2008).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: India–Russia nuclear ties</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Kudankulam Nuclear Power Project</b> (Tamil Nadu): six VVER-1000 reactors by Rosatom; units 1 and 2 operating.</li>
<li>Rooppur (Bangladesh): India participates as a third-country partner.</li>
<li>Other partners: USA (123 Agreement, 2008; Kovvada, AP, Westinghouse), France (Jaitapur, Maharashtra, EDF), Japan (civil nuclear deal 2016), Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Canada and Australia (uranium supply).</li>
<li>India–Russia: S-400 deal (2018), Chennai–Vladivostok Eastern Maritime Corridor, INSTC, rupee-rouble trade, discounted Russian crude after 2022.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-90", year: 2019, no: 90, subject: "economy", topic: "Money multiplier",
  q: `The money multiplier in an economy increases with which one of the following?`,
  opts: ["Increase in the cash reserve ratio", "Increase in the banking habit of the population", "Increase in the statutory liquidity ratio", "Increase in the population of the country"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Increase in the banking habit of the population</h3>
<p>The same idea was asked again in 2021 (Q11). When people keep more of their money as bank deposits and less as cash, banks have more reserves to lend, and each loan creates new deposits. The money multiplier (M3 ÷ M0) rises.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(c)</b> higher CRR or SLR lock up more funds, so the multiplier <b>falls</b>.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> population size has no direct effect.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Simple multiplier = 1 / reserve ratio. With a currency-deposit ratio c and reserve ratio r: multiplier = (1 + c) / (c + r).</li>
<li>Financial inclusion (Jan Dhan, UPI, digital payments) raises banking habit and thus the multiplier.</li>
<li>Demonetisation (2016) temporarily raised deposits and the multiplier, as cash was deposited.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-91", year: 2019, no: 91, subject: "science", topic: "AR and VR",
  q: `In the context of digital technologies for entertainment, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>In Augmented Reality (AR), a simulated environment is created and the physical world is completely shut out.</li><li>In Virtual Reality (VR), images generated from a computer are projected onto real-life objects or surroundings.</li><li>AR allows individuals to be present in the world and improves the experience using the camera of smart-phone or PC.</li><li>VR closes the world, and transposes an individual, providing complete immersion experience.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3", "4 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3 and 4</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> that describes <b>VR</b>, not AR.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> projecting computer images onto real surroundings is <b>AR</b>, not VR.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> <b>AR</b> overlays digital content on the real world, usually through a phone camera or smart glasses (Pokémon Go, Snapchat filters, IKEA Place).</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> <b>VR</b> shuts out the real world with a headset (Meta Quest) and immerses the user in a simulated environment.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: extended reality (XR)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>AR</td><td>Digital overlay on the real world</td></tr>
<tr><td>VR</td><td>Fully virtual, immersive world</td></tr>
<tr><td>MR (Mixed Reality)</td><td>Virtual objects interact with the real world (Microsoft HoloLens, Apple Vision Pro)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metaverse</td><td>Shared, persistent 3D virtual spaces</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Uses: training (surgery, pilots, soldiers), education, tourism, retail, gaming. India's AVGC-XR (Animation, VFX, Gaming, Comics and Extended Reality) promotion.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-92", year: 2019, no: 92, subject: "science", topic: "Denisovans",
  q: `The word 'Denisovan' is sometimes mentioned in media in reference to`,
  opts: ["fossils of a kind of dinosaurs", "an early human species", "a cave system found in North-East India", "a geological period in the history of Indian subcontinent"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) an early human species</h3>
<p><b>Denisovans</b> are an extinct archaic human group, first identified in <b>2010</b> from DNA in a finger bone found in <b>Denisova Cave</b>, Altai Mountains, Siberia. They interbred with Neanderthals and modern humans; their DNA survives in people of Melanesia, Papua New Guinea, Aboriginal Australians, and some South and East Asians. The <b>EPAS1</b> gene that helps Tibetans live at high altitude came from Denisovans. A 2019 jawbone from Tibet (Xiahe) and a 2025 study linking the Harbin skull to Denisovans were in the news.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Denisovans are not dinosaurs.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Denisova Cave is in the Altai Mountains of Siberia, not north-east India.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> It is not a geological period.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: human evolution</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Species</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Australopithecus</td><td>"Lucy" (Ethiopia, 3.2 million years)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo habilis</td><td>First tool maker</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo erectus</td><td>First to leave Africa; used fire</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo neanderthalensis</td><td>Europe and West Asia; extinct about 40,000 years ago</td></tr>
<tr><td>Denisovans</td><td>Asia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo floresiensis</td><td>"Hobbit", Indonesia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Homo sapiens</td><td>Africa, about 300,000 years ago</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>2022 Nobel in Physiology or Medicine: <b>Svante Pääbo</b> for sequencing the Neanderthal genome and discovering Denisovans. India: <b>Hathnora</b> (Narmada valley) skull of archaic Homo.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-93", year: 2019, no: 93, subject: "science", topic: "Synthetic biology",
  q: `With reference to the recent developments in science, which one of the following statements is not correct?`,
  opts: ["Functional chromosomes can be created by joining segments of DNA taken from cells of different species.", "Pieces of artificial functional DNA can be created in laboratories.", "A piece of DNA taken out from an animal cell can be made to replicate outside a living cell in a laboratory.", "Cells taken out from plants and animals can be made to undergo cell division in laboratory petri dishes."], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Functional chromosomes can be created by joining segments of DNA taken from cells of different species.</h3>
<p>UPSC's key treats (a) as the incorrect statement: a functional chromosome needs a centromere, telomeres and origins of replication compatible with the host cell, and simply joining DNA segments from different species does not make a working chromosome.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> correct: DNA can be chemically synthesised (Craig Venter's synthetic bacterial genome, 2010; synthetic yeast chromosomes, Sc2.0 project completed 2023).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> correct: <b>PCR</b> (Kary Mullis, 1983; Nobel 1993) copies DNA in a test tube.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> correct: <b>cell and tissue culture</b> grows cells in petri dishes (HeLa cells; plant micropropagation).</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: artificial chromosomes (YACs, BACs, human artificial chromosomes) are built from DNA of different origins and do work in host cells, so some argue (a) is also true. UPSC kept (a) as "not correct".</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recombinant DNA: Cohen and Boyer (1973). Restriction enzymes cut DNA; ligase joins it; plasmids carry it.</li>
<li>Synthetic biology applications: engineered microbes for insulin, vaccines, biofuels. India's <b>BioE3 policy</b> (2024) for bio-manufacturing.</li>
<li>India's <b>Genome India Project</b> sequenced 10,000 genomes (data released January 2025).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-94", year: 2019, no: 94, subject: "science", topic: "Digital signatures",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
A digital signature is
<ol><li>an electronic record that identifies the certifying authority issuing it</li><li>used to serve as a proof of identity of an individual to access information or server on Internet</li><li>an electronic method of signing an electronic document and ensuring that the original content is unchanged</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> a <b>digital signature</b> uses asymmetric cryptography (private key to sign, public key to verify) to authenticate the signer and ensure the document has not been altered (integrity, non-repudiation).</li>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> that describes a <b>digital certificate</b> (Digital Signature Certificate), which is issued by a Certifying Authority and identifies it, not the signature itself.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> proof of identity to access a server is done by a digital certificate (e.g. SSL/TLS client certificate), not the signature.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>IT Act, 2000</b> gives legal recognition to digital and electronic signatures (Section 3, 3A). <b>Controller of Certifying Authorities</b> (CCA) licenses CAs such as eMudhra, (n)Code, NSDL, Capricorn.</li>
<li>Classes of DSC (now Class 3 only) used for MCA filings, income tax, GST, e-tenders.</li>
<li><b>Aadhaar eSign</b>: online electronic signature using Aadhaar OTP/biometric.</li>
<li>Hashing (SHA-256) creates a fingerprint of the document, which is then encrypted with the private key.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-95", year: 2019, no: 95, subject: "science", topic: "Wearable technology",
  q: `In the context of wearable technology, which of the following tasks is/are accomplished by wearable devices?
<ol><li>Location identification of a person</li><li>Sleep monitoring of a person</li><li>Assisting the hearing impaired person</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1:</b> smartwatches and trackers with <b>GPS</b> identify location (child and elderly trackers, fitness route mapping).</li>
<li><b>2:</b> fitness bands and smart rings use accelerometers and heart-rate sensors to track sleep stages.</li>
<li><b>3:</b> <b>hearing aids</b> and cochlear implants are wearable; some earbuds now have hearing-aid features (Apple AirPods Pro, approved by US FDA in 2024).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other uses: heart rate, ECG and atrial fibrillation detection, blood oxygen (SpO<sub>2</sub>), fall detection, continuous glucose monitoring, contactless payments.</li>
<li>India is one of the largest wearables markets (PLI scheme for electronics, "Make in India" brands).</li>
<li>Privacy concerns: health data under the DPDP Act, 2023.</li>
<li>Internet of Things (IoT) and edge computing underpin wearables.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-96", year: 2019, no: 96, subject: "science", topic: "RNA interference",
  q: `'RNA interference (RNAi)' technology has gained popularity in the last few years. Why?
<ol><li>It is used in developing gene silencing therapies.</li><li>It can be used in developing therapies for the treatment of cancer.</li><li>It can be used to develop hormone replacement therapies.</li><li>It can be used to produce crop plants that are resistant to viral pathogens.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 4", "2 and 3", "1 and 3", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 4</h3>
<p><b>RNAi</b> is a natural mechanism in which small double-stranded RNA (siRNA, miRNA) binds to a matching messenger RNA and causes it to be destroyed, <b>silencing</b> that gene. Andrew Fire and Craig Mello won the <b>2006 Nobel Prize</b> for discovering it (in the worm C. elegans).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>1:</b> gene silencing drugs: <b>patisiran</b> (first RNAi drug, 2018, for hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis), inclisiran (cholesterol).</li>
<li><b>2:</b> silencing oncogenes is being studied for cancer therapy.</li>
<li><b>4:</b> RNAi crops resist viruses and pests (virus-resistant papaya in Hawaii; SmartStax PRO maize against rootworm).</li>
<li><b>3:</b> RNAi switches genes <b>off</b>; it cannot replace missing hormones, so it is not used for hormone replacement therapy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>2024 Nobel in Physiology or Medicine: <b>Victor Ambros and Gary Ruvkun</b> for discovering <b>microRNA</b>.</li>
<li>Related techniques: antisense oligonucleotides, CRISPR (edits DNA permanently, whereas RNAi acts on mRNA, temporarily).</li>
<li>RNAi pesticides (sprayable dsRNA) are an emerging green alternative.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-97", year: 2019, no: 97, subject: "science", topic: "Black hole mergers and gravitational waves",
  q: `Recently, scientists observed the merger of giant 'blackholes' billions of light-years away from the Earth. What is the significance of this observation?`,
  opts: ["'Higgs boson particles' were detected.", "'Gravitational waves' were detected.", "Possibility of inter-galactic space travel through 'wormhole' was confirmed.", "It enabled the scientists to understand 'singularity'."], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 'Gravitational waves' were detected.</h3>
<p>When two black holes spiral into each other and merge, they send out ripples in spacetime called <b>gravitational waves</b>, predicted by Einstein's general relativity (1916). <b>LIGO</b> first detected them on <b>14 September 2015</b> (announced February 2016) from a merger about 1.3 billion light-years away. In 2019 LIGO-Virgo reported the largest merger then known (GW170729, about 5 billion light-years away).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> the Higgs boson was found at CERN's Large Hadron Collider in 2012 (Nobel 2013 to Higgs and Englert).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> wormholes are theoretical; none confirmed.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> singularities are hidden behind event horizons; mergers do not reveal them.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>2017 Nobel in Physics: Rainer Weiss, Barry Barish and Kip Thorne for LIGO.</li>
<li>August 2017: first neutron-star merger (GW170817) seen in both gravitational waves and light ("multi-messenger astronomy").</li>
<li><b>LIGO-India</b>: approved April 2023 at Aundha (Hingoli, Maharashtra), with DAE and DST.</li>
<li>2019: first image of a black hole (M87*) by the Event Horizon Telescope; 2022: Sagittarius A* (Milky Way centre).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-98", year: 2019, no: 98, subject: "science", topic: "Antimicrobial resistance",
  q: `Which of the following are the reasons for the occurrence of multi-drug resistance in microbial pathogens in India?
<ol><li>Genetic predisposition of some people</li><li>Taking incorrect doses of antibiotics to cure diseases</li><li>Using antibiotics in livestock farming</li><li>Multiple chronic diseases in some people</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4", "2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>2 (yes):</b> incomplete courses, wrong doses, self-medication and over-the-counter sales let resistant bacteria survive and multiply.</li>
<li><b>3 (yes):</b> antibiotics used as growth promoters in poultry, cattle and fish farms spread resistant bacteria and genes through food, water and soil.</li>
<li><b>1 (no):</b> resistance develops in the microbes, not because of the human host's genes.</li>
<li><b>4 (no):</b> chronic diseases make people more vulnerable to infections but do not themselves cause drug resistance.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: AMR in India</h3>
<ul>
<li>India is among the largest consumers of antibiotics; <b>NDM-1</b> ("New Delhi metallo-beta-lactamase") superbug gene first reported in 2008.</li>
<li><b>National Action Plan on AMR</b> (2017; NAP-AMR 2.0 in 2025), One Health approach. <b>Red Line campaign</b> (2016): prescription-only antibiotics marked with a red line. Schedule H1 drugs.</li>
<li>FSSAI limits antibiotic residues; colistin banned in animal feed and as growth promoter (2019).</li>
<li>Kerala's <b>KARSAP</b> plan; Kerala's "Operation AMRITH" against OTC antibiotic sales.</li>
<li>Global: WHO GLASS surveillance; UNGA political declaration on AMR (2024): target to cut AMR deaths 10% by 2030.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-99", year: 2019, no: 99, subject: "science", topic: "Cas9 protein",
  q: `What is Cas9 protein that is often mentioned in news?`,
  opts: ["A molecular scissors used in targeted gene editing", "A biosensor used in the accurate detection of pathogens in patients", "A gene that makes plants pest-resistant", "A herbicidal substance synthesized in genetically modified crops"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A molecular scissors used in targeted gene editing</h3>
<p><b>Cas9</b> (CRISPR-associated protein 9) is an enzyme from bacteria (originally <i>Streptococcus pyogenes</i>) that cuts DNA. Guided by a short <b>guide RNA</b> matching the target sequence, it makes a precise double-strand cut; the cell's repair then deletes, alters or inserts genes. Bacteria use CRISPR-Cas naturally as an immune system against viruses.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> CRISPR-based tests (e.g. FELUDA) use Cas enzymes, but Cas9 itself is the cutting enzyme, not a biosensor.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Pest resistance comes from genes like Bt Cry.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Herbicide tolerance uses genes like bar or EPSPS, not Cas9.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: CRISPR</h3>
<ul>
<li>2020 Nobel in Chemistry: <b>Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna</b>.</li>
<li>Variants: Cas12, Cas13 (RNA), <b>base editing</b>, <b>prime editing</b> (more precise, no double-strand break).</li>
<li>Indian uses: <b>FELUDA</b> (CRISPR-based COVID test by CSIR-IGIB, 2020, named after Satyajit Ray's detective); genome-edited rice varieties (2025); sickle cell gene therapy research.</li>
<li>First approved CRISPR therapy: <b>Casgevy</b> (2023) for sickle cell disease and beta-thalassaemia. First personalised CRISPR therapy for an infant (USA, 2025).</li>
<li>Regulation: SDN-1 and SDN-2 edits (no foreign DNA) exempted from GM rules in India (2022).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2019-100", year: 2019, no: 100, subject: "science", topic: "Hepatitis B and C",
  q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct?`,
  opts: ["Hepatitis B virus is transmitted much like HIV.", "Hepatitis B, unlike Hepatitis C, does not have a vaccine.", "Globally, the number of people infected with Hepatitis B and C viruses are several times more than those infected with HIV.", "Some of those infected with Hepatitis B and C viruses do not show the symptoms for many years."], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Hepatitis B, unlike Hepatitis C, does not have a vaccine.</h3>
<p>It is the reverse: <b>Hepatitis B has a safe, effective vaccine</b> (since 1982; part of India's Universal Immunisation Programme, birth dose + three doses in the pentavalent vaccine). <b>Hepatitis C has no vaccine</b>, but it can be <b>cured</b> with direct-acting antivirals (sofosbuvir, daclatasvir) in 12 weeks.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> correct: HBV spreads through blood, sexual contact and mother to child at birth, like HIV (but HBV is far more infectious).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> correct: about 254 million people have chronic Hepatitis B and 50 million Hepatitis C (WHO 2024), versus about 40 million with HIV.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> correct: both cause silent chronic infection for years before cirrhosis or liver cancer.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: viral hepatitis</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Spread</th><th>Vaccine</th></tr>
<tr><td>A, E</td><td>Faecal-oral (contaminated water, food)</td><td>A: yes; E: yes in China only</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>Blood, sexual, mother to child</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>C</td><td>Blood (unsafe injections, transfusions)</td><td>No (curable)</td></tr>
<tr><td>D</td><td>Only with Hepatitis B</td><td>Hep B vaccine protects</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>World Hepatitis Day: <b>28 July</b> (birthday of Baruch Blumberg, discoverer of HBV). <b>National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme</b> (2018). WHO goal: eliminate viral hepatitis as a public health threat by 2030. 2020 Nobel in Medicine: discovery of Hepatitis C virus (Alter, Houghton, Rice).</p>` }

);
