{ id: "2017-1", year: 2017, no: 1, subject: "polity", topic: "Private member's bills",
  q: `With reference to the Parliament of India, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>A private member's bill is a bill presented by a Member of Parliament who is not elected but only nominated by the President of India.</li><li>Recently, a private member's bill has been passed in the Parliament of India for the first time in its history.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> a <b>private member</b> is any MP who is <b>not a minister</b>, elected or nominated. A bill introduced by such a member is a private member's bill; one introduced by a minister is a government bill.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> <b>14 private members' bills</b> have become law, the last in <b>1970</b> (Supreme Court (Enlargement of Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction) Act). In 2015 the Rajya Sabha passed Tiruchi Siva's <b>Rights of Transgender Persons Bill</b>, the first private member's bill passed by either House in 45 years, but it did not become law.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Government bill</th><th>Private member's bill</th></tr>
<tr><td>Introduced by a minister; reflects government policy</td><td>Introduced by a non-minister MP; reflects the member's or opposition's view</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can be introduced any day; 7 days' notice</td><td>Only on Fridays (afternoon); one month's notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drafted by the ministry with the Law Ministry</td><td>Drafted by the member</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rejection may imply loss of confidence</td><td>Rejection has no such effect</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Committee on Private Members' Bills and Resolutions (Lok Sabha) classifies bills and allots time.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-2", year: 2017, no: 2, subject: "ancient", topic: "Rigvedic vs Harappan culture",
  q: `With reference to the difference between the culture of Rigvedic Aryans and Indus Valley people, which of the following statements is/are correct?
<ol><li>Rigvedic Aryans used the coat of mail and helmet in warfare whereas the people of Indus Valley Civilization did not leave any evidence of using them.</li><li>Rigvedic Aryans knew gold, silver and copper whereas Indus Valley people knew only copper and iron.</li><li>Rigvedic Aryans had domesticated the horse whereas there is no evidence of Indus Valley people having been aware of this animal.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the Rigveda mentions <b>varman</b> (coat of mail) and <b>shipra</b> (helmet). No such defensive armour has been found at Harappan sites.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Harappans knew copper, bronze, gold, silver, lead, tin, but <b>not iron</b>. Rigvedic people used <b>ayas</b> (copper or bronze); iron (krishna ayas, shyama ayas) appears only in the Later Vedic period.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct per key):</b> the horse was central to Rigvedic life (chariots, Ashvamedha). The Harappan civilisation was not horse-centred.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Statement 3 is debated: horse bones are claimed at <b>Surkotada</b> (Gujarat) and a terracotta horse at Lothal and Mohenjodaro, but these are disputed and no horse appears on Harappan seals. UPSC accepted statement 3.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes: Harappan vs Vedic</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Harappan</th><th>Rigvedic</th></tr>
<tr><td>Urban, planned cities</td><td>Rural, pastoral</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bronze Age; no iron</td><td>Copper/bronze; iron later</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bull, unicorn on seals; mother goddess, Pashupati</td><td>Indra, Agni, Varuna; cow as wealth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Script undeciphered</td><td>Oral Sanskrit hymns</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trade with Mesopotamia</td><td>Little trade; barter</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-3", year: 2017, no: 3, subject: "economy", topic: "Recognition of Prior Learning",
  q: `'Recognition of Prior Learning Scheme' is sometimes mentioned in the news with reference to`,
  opts: ["Certifying the skills acquired by construction workers through traditional channels.", "Enrolling the persons in Universities for distance learning programmes.", "Reserving some skilled jobs to rural and urban poor in some public sector undertakings.", "Certifying the skills acquired by trainees under the National Skill Development Programme."], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Certifying the skills acquired by construction workers through traditional channels.</h3>
<p><b>Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL)</b>, a component of <b>PMKVY</b>, assesses and certifies the skills that workers have gained informally (on the job, from family trades) against the <b>National Skills Qualification Framework (NSQF)</b>. Construction workers were a major target group (with the Ministry of Labour and Construction Skill Development Council), so they can get better wages and mobility.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(d)</b> refers to trainees of formal programmes, who are certified after training; RPL is for skills acquired <b>before</b> or outside formal training.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Distance learning enrolment is done through IGNOU and open universities, not RPL.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> RPL does not reserve jobs; it certifies existing skills.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's workforce is about 90% informal; only a small share has formal vocational training (PLFS).</li>
<li>RPL types: Camp-based, RPL at employer's premises, RPL centres, Best-in-class employers.</li>
<li>NSQF (2013): 10 levels from Class 5 equivalence to doctorate; allows mobility between vocational and general education (asked 2017 Q61).</li>
<li>Skill India Digital Hub (2023) issues digital skill credentials.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-4", year: 2017, no: 4, subject: "environment", topic: "Eastern–Western Ghats corridor",
  q: `From the ecological point of view, which one of the following assumes importance in being a good link between the Eastern Ghats and the Western Ghats?`,
  opts: ["Sathyamangalam Tiger Reserve", "Nallamala Forest", "Nagarhole National Park", "Seshachalam Biosphere Reserve"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Sathyamangalam Tiger Reserve</h3>
<p><b>Sathyamangalam</b> (Erode, Tamil Nadu; tiger reserve since 2013) lies where the Western Ghats meet the Eastern Ghats. It connects the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve (Mudumalai, Bandipur, BRT Hills, MM Hills) with Eastern Ghats forests, forming a key corridor for <b>tigers and elephants</b> (the largest Asian elephant population). The Moyar river and Moyar gorge are here.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Nallamala</b> (AP and Telangana) and <b>Seshachalam</b> (Chittoor, red sanders) are in the Eastern Ghats only.</li>
<li><b>Nagarhole</b> is in the Western Ghats (Karnataka).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Nallamala lies entirely in the Eastern Ghats (AP, Telangana).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Nagarhole lies in the Western Ghats (Karnataka).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Seshachalam (Chittoor, AP) is in the Eastern Ghats; known for red sanders.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>BRT (Biligiri Ranganathaswamy Temple) Tiger Reserve</b> (Karnataka) is also a bridge between the two Ghats; Soliga tribe got forest rights inside a tiger reserve (2011).</li>
<li>Eastern Ghats: discontinuous, older, lower; highest peak Jindhagada/Arma Konda (AP). Western Ghats: continuous, WHS (2012), biodiversity hotspot; highest peak Anamudi.</li>
<li>Elephant corridors: about 150 identified (Elephant Corridors of India report, 2023).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-5", year: 2017, no: 5, subject: "polity", topic: "Equality",
  q: `One of the implications of equality in society is the absence of`,
  opts: ["Privileges", "Restraints", "Competition", "Ideology"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Privileges</h3>
<p>Equality means no individual or group enjoys <b>special privileges</b> by birth, caste, class, religion or gender; all have equal opportunities. Harold Laski described equality as, first, "the absence of special privileges" and, second, "adequate opportunities laid open to all".</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Restraints</b> (laws) apply equally to all, so equality does not mean their absence.</li>
<li><b>Competition</b> can exist on equal terms (equality of opportunity).</li>
<li><b>Ideology</b> is irrelevant here.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: equality in the Constitution</h3>
<ul>
<li>Preamble: equality of status and opportunity.</li>
<li>Art 14 (equality before law), 15 (no discrimination), 16 (equal opportunity in public employment), 17 (abolition of untouchability), 18 (abolition of titles; this directly removes privileges).</li>
<li>Kinds: formal (legal) equality and substantive equality (affirmative action for disadvantaged groups through Art 15(4), 16(4)).</li>
<li>Types: political, social, economic equality; equality of opportunity vs equality of outcome.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-6", year: 2017, no: 6, subject: "environment", topic: "TRAFFIC",
  q: `Consider the following statements in respect of Trade Related Analysis of Fauna and Flora in Commerce (TRAFFIC):
<ol><li>TRAFFIC is a bureau under United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP).</li><li>The mission of TRAFFIC is to ensure that trade in wild plants and animals is not a threat to the conservation of nature.</li></ol>
Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> TRAFFIC (1976, HQ Cambridge, UK) is a non-governmental network set up as a joint programme of <b>WWF and IUCN</b>, not a UNEP bureau. In India it works through WWF-India.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> its mission is to ensure that trade in wild plants and animals is not a threat to the conservation of nature; it supports enforcement of CITES.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: wildlife trade control</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>CITES</b> (1973, Washington; secretariat Geneva, administered by UNEP): Appendix I (trade banned except special cases), II (trade regulated), III (protected in one country). India joined 1976. CoP20 was held in Uzbekistan (2025).</li>
<li>India: <b>Wildlife Crime Control Bureau</b> (WCCB, 2007, under WPA) coordinates enforcement; Operation Save Kurma (turtles), Operation Thunderbird.</li>
<li>Most trafficked: pangolins, tortoises, star tortoise, red sanders, tiger parts, rhino horn, ivory, shark fins.</li>
<li>WPA amendment 2022 brought CITES species into Schedule IV.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-7", year: 2017, no: 7, subject: "polity", topic: "DPSPs added by the 42nd Amendment",
  q: `Which principle among the following was added to the Directive Principles of State Policy by the 42nd Amendment to the Constitution?`,
  opts: ["Equal pay for equal work for both men and women", "Participation of workers in the management of industries", "Right to work, education and public assistance", "Securing living wage and human conditions of work to workers"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Participation of workers in the management of industries</h3>
<p>The <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b> added four DPSPs:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Art 39(f)</b>: opportunities for healthy development of children.</li>
<li><b>Art 39A</b>: equal justice and free legal aid.</li>
<li><b>Art 43A</b>: <b>participation of workers in management of industries</b>.</li>
<li><b>Art 48A</b>: protection and improvement of environment, forests and wildlife.</li>
</ul>
<p>The other options were in the original Constitution: equal pay (Art 39(d)), right to work, education and public assistance (Art 41), living wage and humane conditions (Art 43, and Art 42 for maternity relief).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Equal pay for equal work was in the original Constitution (Art 39(d)).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Living wage and humane conditions were in the original Constitution (Art 43, 42).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: later DPSP amendments</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Change</th></tr>
<tr><td>44th (1978)</td><td>Art 38(2): minimise inequalities in income, status, opportunities</td></tr>
<tr><td>86th (2002)</td><td>Art 45 changed to early childhood care and education below 6 years; Art 21A made education a FR; Art 51A(k) duty of parents</td></tr>
<tr><td>97th (2011)</td><td>Art 43B: promotion of cooperative societies</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-8", year: 2017, no: 8, subject: "polity", topic: "Nature of rights",
  q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
  opts: ["Rights are claims of the State against the citizens.", "Rights are privileges which are incorporated in the Constitution of a State.", "Rights are claims of the citizens against the State.", "Rights are privileges of a few citizens against the many."], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Rights are claims of the citizens against the State.</h3>
<p>A right is a <b>justified claim</b> of an individual, recognised by society and enforced by the State, which limits the State's power over the individual. Fundamental Rights in India are mostly claims against the "State" (Article 12) and protect citizens from its arbitrary action.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> the State has powers and claims, but "rights" belong to individuals.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> and <b>(d)</b> rights are not privileges; they are available equally to all.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Laski: "Rights are those conditions of social life without which no man can seek, in general, to be himself at his best."</li>
<li>Types: natural rights (Locke: life, liberty, property), moral rights, legal rights, human rights (UDHR), fundamental rights (constitutional, justiciable).</li>
<li>Rights and duties are correlative (2017 Q47).</li>
<li>Some FRs apply against private persons too: Art 15(2), 17, 23, 24.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-9", year: 2017, no: 9, subject: "economy", topic: "Global Gender Gap Index",
  q: `Which of the following gives 'Global Gender Gap Index' ranking to the countries of the world?`,
  opts: ["World Economic Forum", "UN Human Rights Council", "UN Women", "World Health Organization"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) World Economic Forum</h3>
<p>The WEF's <b>Global Gender Gap Report</b> (since 2006) ranks countries on four sub-indices: <b>economic participation and opportunity</b>, <b>educational attainment</b>, <b>health and survival</b>, and <b>political empowerment</b>. India ranked 131st of 148 in 2025 (129th in 2024); its weakest areas are economic participation and political empowerment. Iceland tops the list.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> The UN Human Rights Council conducts the Universal Periodic Review, not a gender gap index.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> UN Women works on gender equality but does not publish this index.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> WHO publishes health statistics, not the Global Gender Gap Index.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: gender indices</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Index</th><th>Publisher</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gender Development Index, Gender Inequality Index</td><td>UNDP (Human Development Report)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Women, Business and the Law</td><td>World Bank</td></tr>
<tr><td>Women, Peace and Security Index</td><td>Georgetown Institute (GIWPS)</td></tr>
<tr><td>SDG Gender Index</td><td>Equal Measures 2030</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>India's measures: Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (2015), Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam (2023), Mission Shakti (2022), Lakhpati Didi, Stand-Up India.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-10", year: 2017, no: 10, subject: "ca", caYear: "2016–17", topic: "Smart India Hackathon",
  q: `Which of the following statements is/are correct regarding Smart India Hackathon 2017?
<ol><li>It is a centrally sponsored scheme for developing every city of our country into Smart Cities in a decade.</li><li>It is an initiative to identify new digital technology innovations for solving the many problems faced by our country.</li><li>It is a programme aimed at making all the financial transactions in our country completely digital in a decade.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<p><b>Smart India Hackathon</b> (first held April 2017; Ministry of Education/AICTE, with Persistent Systems, i4c and others) is a nationwide non-stop digital product development competition where students solve problem statements posed by ministries, departments and industry. It is not a Smart Cities scheme or a payments digitisation programme.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Smart Cities Mission (2015) is a separate scheme; the Hackathon is not about building smart cities.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> It is a competition to find digital solutions to problems posed by ministries and industry.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Making all transactions digital is not its aim (that relates to Digital India and payments policy).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: innovation initiatives</h3>
<ul>
<li>SIH now has software and hardware editions; SIH Junior for school students.</li>
<li>Related: <b>Atal Innovation Mission</b> (NITI Aayog, 2016), <b>Startup India</b> (2016), <b>Smart Cities Mission</b> (2015, 100 cities, MoHUA, Centrally sponsored), <b>Digital India</b> (2015), <b>Institution's Innovation Councils</b> (MoE), <b>KAPILA</b> (IP literacy).</li>
<li>Toycathon (2021), Kavach hackathon (cyber security, 2021).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-11", year: 2017, no: 11, subject: "economy", topic: "Monetary Policy Committee",
  q: `Which of the following statements is/are correct regarding the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)?
<ol><li>It decides the RBI's benchmark interest rates.</li><li>It is a 12-member body including the Governor of RBI and is reconstituted every year.</li><li>It functions under the chairmanship of the Union Finance Minister.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the MPC sets the <b>policy repo rate</b> to achieve the inflation target.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> it has <b>six</b> members: RBI Governor (chair), a Deputy Governor, one RBI officer nominated by the Board, and <b>three external members</b> appointed by the Centre (via a search committee headed by the Cabinet Secretary) for <b>four years</b>, not reappointable. It is not reconstituted every year.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> it is chaired by the <b>RBI Governor</b>, who has a casting vote in a tie.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: monetary policy framework</h3>
<ul>
<li>Based on the <b>Urjit Patel Committee</b> (2014); RBI Act amended 2016 (Section 45ZB); first MPC meeting October 2016.</li>
<li><b>Flexible inflation targeting</b>: CPI inflation of <b>4% ±2%</b> (tolerance band 2–6%), set by the Centre every five years (retained for 2021–26 and again for <b>2026–31</b>, notified 25 March 2026).</li>
<li>Meets at least four times a year (in practice six, bimonthly); quorum four; minutes published on the 14th day.</li>
<li>If inflation is outside the band for three consecutive quarters, the RBI must report reasons to the government (first happened in 2022).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-12", year: 2017, no: 12, subject: "culture", topic: "Manipuri Sankirtana",
  q: `With reference to Manipuri Sankirtana, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>It is a song and dance performance.</li><li>Cymbals are the only musical instruments used in the performance.</li><li>It is performed to narrate the life and deeds of Lord Krishna.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<p><b>Sankirtana</b> of Manipur, inscribed on UNESCO's <b>Intangible Cultural Heritage</b> list in <b>2013</b>, is ritual singing, drumming and dancing performed in temples and homes at religious occasions and life-cycle ceremonies of the Vaishnava people of the Manipur valley.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> it combines singing and dancing.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> besides cymbals (kartal), the <b>pung</b> (drum) is central; performers dance while playing them.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> it narrates the life and deeds of <b>Krishna</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: India's UNESCO ICH elements (selection)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Element</th><th>Year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Koodiyattam, Ramlila, Vedic chanting</td><td>2008</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ramman (Garhwal), Mudiyettu, Kalbelia, Chhau</td><td>2009–10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buddhist chanting of Ladakh, Sankirtana</td><td>2012–13</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thatheras of Jandiala Guru (brass utensils)</td><td>2014</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yoga, Nowruz</td><td>2016</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kumbh Mela</td><td>2017</td></tr>
<tr><td>Durga Puja in Kolkata</td><td>2021</td></tr>
<tr><td>Garba of Gujarat</td><td>2023</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deepavali</td><td>2025</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-13", year: 2017, no: 13, subject: "modern", topic: "Ryotwari Settlement",
  q: `Who among the following was/were associated with the introduction of Ryotwari Settlement in India during the British rule?
<ol><li>Lord Cornwallis</li><li>Alexander Read</li><li>Thomas Munro</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<p>The <b>Ryotwari system</b> was first tried by <b>Captain Alexander Read</b> in the Baramahal region (Salem, 1792) and extended by <b>Thomas Munro</b> (Governor of Madras, 1820). Revenue was settled directly with each cultivator (ryot), who got ownership rights; rates were high (about half of net produce initially) and revised every 20–30 years. It covered Madras and Bombay Presidencies, Assam, Coorg (about 51% of British India's land).</p>
<p><b>Lord Cornwallis</b> introduced the <b>Permanent Settlement</b> (1793) in Bengal, Bihar, Orissa with zamindars.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: land revenue systems</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>System</th><th>Who</th><th>Where</th><th>Settled with</th></tr>
<tr><td>Permanent Settlement</td><td>Cornwallis (John Shore helped), 1793</td><td>Bengal, Bihar, Odisha, Benares</td><td>Zamindars; fixed in perpetuity; "sunset law"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ryotwari</td><td>Read, Munro (1820); Elphinstone in Bombay</td><td>Madras, Bombay, Assam</td><td>Ryots</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mahalwari</td><td>Holt Mackenzie (1822); revised by Bird and Thomason (1833)</td><td>NW Provinces, Punjab, Central India</td><td>Village community (mahal)</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-14", year: 2017, no: 14, subject: "environment", topic: "Bioremediation",
  q: `In the context of solving pollution problems, what is/are the advantage/advantages of bioremediation technique?
<ol><li>It is a technique for cleaning up pollution by enhancing the same biodegradation process that occurs in nature.</li><li>Any contaminant with heavy metals such as cadmium and lead can be readily and completely treated by bioremediation using microorganisms.</li><li>Genetic engineering can be used to create microorganisms specifically designed for bioremediation.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> bioremediation uses microbes, fungi or plants to break down pollutants, speeding up natural biodegradation (adding nutrients, oxygen, or microbes).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> heavy metals like cadmium, lead, mercury <b>cannot be degraded</b>; microbes can only change their form or concentrate them (biosorption, bioaccumulation). Treatment is not ready or complete.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> genetically engineered microbes can be designed to degrade specific pollutants. The first patented GM organism (Ananda Chakrabarty's oil-eating <i>Pseudomonas</i>, Diamond v. Chakrabarty, 1980) was for oil spill cleanup.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: types</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>In situ</td><td>At the site (bioventing, biosparging)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ex situ</td><td>Removed and treated (landfarming, biopiles, bioreactors)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phytoremediation</td><td>Plants absorb or degrade pollutants (sunflower for radioactive caesium after Chernobyl; Indian mustard for heavy metals)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mycoremediation</td><td>Fungi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bioaugmentation / biostimulation</td><td>Add microbes / add nutrients</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>India: <b>Oilzapper</b> (TERI and IOC) bacteria cocktail for oil sludge; bioremediation of legacy waste dumps and drains (Namami Gange).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-15", year: 2017, no: 15, subject: "modern", topic: "Trade Disputes Act, 1929",
  q: `The Trade Disputes Act of 1929 provided for`,
  opts: ["the participation of workers in the management of industries.", "arbitrary powers to the management to quell industrial disputes.", "an intervention by the British Court in the event of a trade dispute.", "a system of tribunals and a ban on strikes."], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) a system of tribunals and a ban on strikes.</h3>
<p>The <b>Trade Disputes Act, 1929</b> (along with the Public Safety Bill) was passed amid rising labour militancy (Bombay textile strikes, communist-led unions). It provided for <b>Courts of Inquiry and Boards of Conciliation</b> to settle disputes, and made strikes in <b>public utility services</b> illegal without notice, as well as strikes intended to coerce the government. The government then launched the <b>Meerut Conspiracy Case</b> (1929) against trade union leaders.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Workers' participation in management came later (Art 43A, 1976).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> It did not give management arbitrary powers; it set up inquiry and conciliation machinery.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> It did not provide for intervention by a British court.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: labour movement in British India</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th></tr>
<tr><td>1881</td><td>First Factories Act (child labour)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1884–90</td><td>N.M. Lokhande: Bombay Mill Hands Association (1890)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1918</td><td>Madras Labour Union (B.P. Wadia); Ahmedabad mill strike (Gandhi)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1920</td><td>AITUC founded (Lala Lajpat Rai president)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1926</td><td>Trade Unions Act (registration, immunity)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1928</td><td>Girni Kamgar Union (Bombay, communist)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1929</td><td>Trade Disputes Act; Meerut Conspiracy Case; Royal Commission on Labour (Whitley)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1947</td><td>Industrial Disputes Act</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-16", year: 2017, no: 16, subject: "polity", topic: "Local self-government",
  q: `Local self-government can be best explained as an exercise in`,
  opts: ["Federalism", "Democratic decentralisation", "Administrative delegation", "Direct democracy"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Democratic decentralisation</h3>
<p>Local self-government transfers power, functions and funds to <b>elected local bodies</b> (panchayats, municipalities), so people govern themselves at the grassroots. The term "<b>democratic decentralisation</b>" was used by the <b>Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957)</b>, which recommended three-tier Panchayati Raj.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Federalism</b> is the division of power between Centre and States, constitutionally protected.</li>
<li><b>Administrative delegation</b> (deconcentration) transfers work to subordinate officials, not to elected bodies.</li>
<li><b>Direct democracy</b> (people deciding directly) exists only partly through Gram Sabhas.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Panchayati Raj committees</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Committee</th><th>Recommendation</th></tr>
<tr><td>Balwant Rai Mehta (1957)</td><td>Three tiers; first PRIs in Nagaur, Rajasthan (2 October 1959)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ashok Mehta (1977)</td><td>Two tiers; party participation</td></tr>
<tr><td>G.V.K. Rao (1985)</td><td>District as key unit for planning</td></tr>
<tr><td>L.M. Singhvi (1986)</td><td>Constitutional status</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p><b>73rd Amendment (1992)</b>: Part IX, 11th Schedule (29 subjects), one-third seats for women (21 States raised to 50%), State Election Commission, State Finance Commission. National Panchayati Raj Day: 24 April.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-17", year: 2017, no: 17, subject: "polity", topic: "DPSPs as limitations",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
With reference to the Constitution of India, the Directive Principles of State Policy constitute limitations upon
<ol><li>legislative function.</li><li>executive function.</li></ol>
Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<p>DPSPs are <b>positive directions</b> to the State ("instruments of instructions"), not limitations. Under <b>Article 37</b> they are not enforceable by courts; a law or executive action cannot be struck down for violating a DPSP. It is the <b>Fundamental Rights</b> (negative obligations, Article 13) that limit legislative and executive power.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> DPSPs do not limit the legislature; they guide law-making (Art 37). Fundamental Rights limit it.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> DPSPs do not limit the executive either; they are positive directions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>FRs: "Thou shalt not" (limitations on the State). DPSPs: "Thou shalt" (positive obligations).</li>
<li>Courts still use DPSPs to interpret laws and to uphold reasonable restrictions on FRs (e.g. cow slaughter ban upheld with Art 48; State of Gujarat v. Mirzapur Moti Kureshi, 2005).</li>
<li>Art 31C protects laws giving effect to Art 39(b), (c) from challenge under Art 14 and 19.</li>
<li>B.R. Ambedkar: DPSPs are like the Instrument of Instructions under the 1935 Act; their sanction is the electorate.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-18", year: 2017, no: 18, subject: "ir", topic: "EU Digital Single Market",
  q: `The term 'Digital Single Market Strategy' seen in the news refers to`,
  opts: ["ASEAN", "BRICS", "EU", "G20"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) EU</h3>
<p>The <b>European Commission</b> adopted the <b>Digital Single Market Strategy</b> in May 2015 to remove regulatory barriers between member states' online markets, with three pillars: better access to digital goods and services across Europe (end of roaming charges, 2017; ban on geo-blocking, 2018), the right environment for digital networks (telecom rules, GDPR, cyber security), and maximising the growth potential of the digital economy.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> ASEAN has the ASEAN Digital Masterplan, not the Digital Single Market Strategy.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> BRICS has no such strategy.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> G20 discusses the digital economy but this specific strategy is the EU's (2015).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: EU digital laws</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Law</th><th>Year</th><th>Subject</th></tr>
<tr><td>GDPR</td><td>2016 (applied 2018)</td><td>Data protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Digital Services Act</td><td>2022</td><td>Illegal content, platform accountability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Digital Markets Act</td><td>2022</td><td>"Gatekeeper" Big Tech competition</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI Act</td><td>2024</td><td>Risk-based AI regulation (world's first)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data Act</td><td>2023</td><td>Access to data</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>India–EU Trade and Technology Council (2022); India–EU FTA negotiations relaunched in 2022, concluded at the 16th India–EU Summit on 27 January 2026 (signing and EU ratification pending).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-19", year: 2017, no: 19, subject: "geography", topic: "Chandipur: receding sea",
  q: `At one of the places in India, if you stand on the seashore and watch the sea, you will find that the sea water recedes from the shore line a few kilometres and comes back to the shore, twice a day, and you can actually walk on the sea floor when the water recedes. This unique phenomenon is seen at`,
  opts: ["Bhavnagar", "Bheemunipatnam", "Chandipur", "Nagapattinam"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Chandipur</h3>
<p>At <b>Chandipur</b> (Balasore district, <b>Odisha</b>), the very gently sloping beach means that at <b>low tide</b> the sea recedes up to about 5 km, letting people walk on the seabed; it returns at high tide twice a day. It is called the "<b>hide-and-seek beach</b>". The <b>Integrated Test Range</b> of DRDO (missile tests: Agni, Prithvi, Akash, BrahMos) is at Chandipur.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Bhavnagar (Gujarat) on the Gulf of Khambhat has a high tidal range but is not known for walking on the receding sea floor.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: tides</h3>
<ul>
<li>Caused mainly by the Moon's gravity (and Sun's). Most places have two high and two low tides a day (semi-diurnal), about 12 hours 25 minutes apart.</li>
<li><b>Spring tides</b>: new and full moon (Sun, Moon, Earth in line), highest range. <b>Neap tides</b>: first and third quarter, lowest range.</li>
<li>Highest tidal range in India: <b>Gulf of Khambhat</b> and Gulf of Kutch (tidal power potential). World's highest: Bay of Fundy (Canada).</li>
<li>Other sites: <b>Abdul Kalam Island</b> (Wheeler Island, Odisha), missile test site off Chandipur. Bheemunipatnam (AP) is a Dutch-era town; Nagapattinam (TN) was hit by the 2004 tsunami.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-20", year: 2017, no: 20, subject: "economy", topic: "Benami Act",
  q: `With reference to the 'Prohibition of Benami Property Transactions Act, 1988 (PBPT Act)', consider the following statements:
<ol><li>A property transaction is not treated as a benami transaction if the owner of the property is not aware of the transaction.</li><li>Properties held benami are liable for confiscation by the Government.</li><li>The Act provides for three authorities for investigations but does not provide for any appellate mechanism.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the amended Act also covers transactions where the owner is <b>not aware of, or denies knowledge of</b>, ownership of the property.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> benami property is liable to <b>confiscation</b> by the Centre without compensation.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> the Act sets up <b>four</b> authorities: Initiating Officer, Approving Authority, Administrator, and the <b>Adjudicating Authority</b>, and an <b>Appellate Tribunal</b>, with further appeal to the High Court.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>"Benami" = property held in the name of another person while the real owner pays for it.</li>
<li>The original 1988 Act had no rules; the <b>Benami Transactions (Prohibition) Amendment Act, 2016</b> (in force 1 November 2016) made it effective, renamed it PBPT Act.</li>
<li>Penalty: rigorous imprisonment of 1–7 years and fine up to 25% of fair market value; false information: 6 months–5 years.</li>
<li>Exceptions: property in the name of spouse, child, sibling, lineal ascendant/descendant paid from known sources; held by a trustee or fiduciary.</li>
<li>In <b>Ganpati Dealcom (August 2022)</b> the Supreme Court held the 2016 amendment's penal provisions do not apply retrospectively and struck down Section 3(2) of the 1988 Act; the Court later recalled this judgment (October 2024) for fresh hearing.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-21", year: 2017, no: 21, subject: "environment", topic: "Butterflies and food webs",
  q: `Due to some reasons, if there is a huge fall in the population of species of butterflies, what could be its likely consequence/consequences?
<ol><li>Pollination of some plants could be adversely affected.</li><li>There could be a drastic increase in the fungal infections of some cultivated plants.</li><li>It could lead to a fall in the population of some species of wasps, spiders and birds.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> butterflies are pollinators; their loss would harm plants that depend on them.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> butterflies (eggs, caterpillars, adults) are food for wasps (parasitoid wasps lay eggs in caterpillars), spiders, birds, lizards; predators would decline.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> butterflies do not control fungal infections of crops; no direct link.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: butterflies and pollinators</h3>
<ul>
<li>Butterflies are <b>bio-indicators</b> of ecosystem health (sensitive to climate and habitat change).</li>
<li>India has about 1,300+ butterfly species; Western Ghats and northeast richest. <b>Southern Birdwing</b> is the largest Indian butterfly (Western Ghats); <b>Blue Mormon</b> is Maharashtra's State butterfly (first State to declare one, 2015).</li>
<li>Pollinator decline: bees, butterflies, moths, bats, birds; due to pesticides (neonicotinoids), habitat loss, climate change. IPBES pollinators assessment (2016): about 75% of food crops depend partly on animal pollination.</li>
<li>Butterfly migrations: Monarch (North America); Blue Tigers and Crows in the Western Ghats (monsoon migration).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-22", year: 2017, no: 22, subject: "environment", topic: "Algae-based biofuels",
  q: `It is possible to produce algae based biofuels, but what is/are the likely limitation(s) of developing countries in promoting this industry?
<ol><li>Production of algae based biofuels is possible in seas only and not on continents.</li><li>Setting up and engineering the algae based biofuel production requires high level of expertise/technology until the construction is completed.</li><li>Economically viable production necessitates the setting up of large scale facilities which may raise ecological and social concerns.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> algae can be grown on land in <b>open ponds</b> (raceways) or closed <b>photobioreactors</b>, even on wastelands using saline or waste water.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> strain selection, cultivation, harvesting (dewatering), and oil extraction need advanced technology and expertise.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> viable production requires large facilities with high water and nutrient demand, land use and possible ecological effects.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: third-generation biofuels</h3>
<ul>
<li>Advantages: high oil yield per hectare (much more than oilseed crops), no competition with food crops, absorb CO<sub>2</sub>, can use wastewater.</li>
<li>Disadvantages: high cost, energy-intensive harvesting, contamination of open ponds, nutrient needs (nitrogen, phosphorus).</li>
<li>Biofuel generations: 1G (food crops: sugarcane, corn, vegetable oil), 2G (lignocellulosic waste: straw, bagasse), 3G (algae), 4G (GM organisms, carbon capture).</li>
<li>India: National Policy on Biofuels 2018 lists algae among advanced biofuels; DBT supports algae research.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-23", year: 2017, no: 23, subject: "polity", topic: "POSHAN Abhiyaan",
  q: `Which of the following are the objectives of 'National Nutrition Mission'?
<ol><li>To create awareness relating to malnutrition among pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li><li>To reduce the incidence of anaemia among young children, adolescent girls and women.</li><li>To promote the consumption of millets, coarse cereals and unpolished rice.</li><li>To promote the consumption of poultry eggs.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<p>The <b>National Nutrition Mission</b> (renamed <b>POSHAN Abhiyaan</b>, launched 8 March 2018 at Jhunjhunu; Ministry of Women and Child Development) aims to reduce stunting, undernutrition, <b>anaemia</b> (in children, adolescent girls and women) and low birth weight, through convergence, technology (Poshan Tracker), behaviour change and community mobilisation (<b>Jan Andolan</b>). Awareness among pregnant women and lactating mothers is a central objective.</p>
<p>Promoting millets or eggs is not among its stated objectives (though millets were later promoted through Poshan Maah and the International Year of Millets 2023).</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Awareness on malnutrition among pregnant and lactating women is an objective.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Reducing anaemia among children, adolescent girls and women is an objective.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Promoting millets was not among the mission's stated objectives.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> Promoting eggs was not among its stated objectives.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Targets (original): reduce stunting by 2% a year, undernutrition and low birth weight by 2% a year, anaemia by 3% a year.</li>
<li><b>Mission Poshan 2.0</b> (Saksham Anganwadi and Poshan 2.0, 2021): merged ICDS supplementary nutrition, POSHAN Abhiyaan, Scheme for Adolescent Girls.</li>
<li>September is <b>Poshan Maah</b>; March has Poshan Pakhwada.</li>
<li>NFHS-5 (2019–21): stunting 35.5%, wasting 19.3%, underweight 32.1%, anaemia among women 57%. Anaemia Mukt Bharat (2018, 6×6×6 strategy).</li>
<li>Rice fortification in PDS, ICDS and PM POSHAN (completed 2024).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-24", year: 2017, no: 24, subject: "modern", topic: "Early labour laws",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Factories Act, 1881 was passed with a view to fix the wages of industrial workers and to allow the workers to form trade unions.</li><li>N.M. Lokhande was a pioneer in organizing the labour movement in British India.</li></ol>
Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the <b>Factories Act, 1881</b> (Lord Ripon) dealt mainly with <b>child labour</b>: banned employment of children under 7, limited hours of children aged 7–12 to 9 hours, and required fencing of dangerous machinery. It did not fix wages or allow unions. (Trade unions got legal status only under the Trade Unions Act, 1926.)</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>Narayan Meghaji Lokhande</b>, an associate of Jyotiba Phule, organised the <b>Bombay Mill Hands Association</b> (1890), considered India's first labour organisation; he won a weekly holiday (Sunday) for mill workers (1890). He edited Deenbandhu. India Post issued a stamp on him (2005).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Factories Act 1891: minimum age raised to 9; women's hours limited to 11; weekly holiday.</li>
<li>Indian Factories Act 1911: 12-hour day for men. 1934: 10 hours. Factories Act 1948 (post-independence; now part of the OSH Code 2020).</li>
<li>Early workers' organisations: Sasipada Banerjee (Working Men's Club, Bengal, 1870), Printers' Union Calcutta (1905), Postal Union Bombay (1907).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-25", year: 2017, no: 25, subject: "environment", topic: "Carbon sequestration sites",
  q: `In the context of mitigating the impending global warming due to anthropogenic emissions of carbon dioxide, which of the following can be the potential sites for carbon sequestration?
<ol><li>Abandoned and uneconomic coal seams</li><li>Depleted oil and gas reservoirs</li><li>Subterranean deep saline formations</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<p>In <b>Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS)</b>, CO<sub>2</sub> captured at power plants or factories is compressed and injected deep underground into geological formations:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Unmineable coal seams</b>: coal adsorbs CO<sub>2</sub> and can release methane (Enhanced Coal Bed Methane recovery).</li>
<li><b>Depleted oil and gas fields</b>: proven traps; CO<sub>2</sub> injection also boosts oil recovery (EOR).</li>
<li><b>Deep saline aquifers</b>: largest storage capacity worldwide (e.g. Sleipner, Norway since 1996).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other sinks: oceans (risky), basalt formations (mineralisation; Iceland's CarbFix; Deccan basalts studied in India), forests and soils (biological sequestration), blue carbon.</li>
<li><b>CCUS</b> (utilisation): CO<sub>2</sub> to urea, chemicals, building materials. NITI Aayog CCUS policy report (2022). India's CCUS R&amp;D roadmap (DST).</li>
<li>Direct Air Capture (Climeworks, Switzerland). Bioenergy with CCS (BECCS) gives negative emissions.</li>
</ul>` }

